window.SF_STUDY = {"_rule":"distractors are wrong answers only, never the subject of a question. INFERRED facts are dropped at build: teachable, not markable.","built":"2026-10-05T23:29:41","glasses_per_bottle":4,"wines":[{"id":"wine:house-prosecco--bellenda--san-fermo--prosecco-superiore-brut","name":"House Prosecco: Bellenda \"San Fermo\" Prosecco Superiore Brut","section":"Bubbles","type":"sparkling","region":"Conegliano Valdobbiadene DOCG, Veneto","country":"Italy","grapes":["Glera"],"vintage":null,"glass":13,"bottle":50,"status":"core-glass","vendor":"Specialty Cellars","cost":13.33,"cost_markable":true,"cost_note":null,"bottle_cost_pct":26.7,"glass_cost_pct":25.6,"glass_cost_pct_at_5":20.5,"pour":"Luminous, with a dense, continuous bead of pin-point bubbles. Delicate and fragrant on the nose; savory on the palate with a fine vein of minerality, finishing on a crisp walnut-husk tang.","producer_note":"Bellenda's San Fermo is named for the country church next to the vineyard where the grapes grow, in the Carpesica area of the Conegliano hills. The glera vines sit around 180 m on limestone-clay soils rich in morainic deposits left by the ancient Piave glacier.","winemaking":"Grapes de-stemmed and gently pressed; must gravity-settled and fermented at 18-20°C, then 1-3 months on the lees. Second fermentation by the Italian (tank) method in 100 hl steel pressure fermenters for about 2 months. Brut; 11.5% ABV. Serve at about 43°F.","pron":[{"term":"Bellenda","pron":"bel-LEN-dah"},{"term":"Glera","pron":"GLEH-rah"},{"term":"Conegliano","pron":"koh-neh-LYAH-noh"}],"specs":[{"label":"Body","rating":2,"low":"light","high":"full"},{"label":"Dryness","rating":4,"low":"sweet","high":"bone dry"},{"label":"Acidity","rating":3,"low":"soft","high":"bright"},{"label":"Tannins","rating":1,"low":"none","high":"grippy"}]},{"id":"wine:paltrinieri--solco--lambrusco-di-sorbara-doc","name":"Paltrinieri \"Solco\" Lambrusco di Sorbara DOC","section":"Bubbles","type":"sparkling","region":"Sorbara, Emilia-Romagna","country":"Italy","grapes":["Lambrusco di Sorbara"],"vintage":2025,"glass":14,"bottle":55,"status":"core-glass","vendor":"Petit Monde","cost":14.25,"cost_markable":false,"cost_note":"Sheet cost is for Paltrinieri 'Sant'Agata' 2024; the menu prints 'Solco' 2025.","pour":"Vibrant pink with fine, persistent bubbles — fresh raspberry and strawberry, a lift of violet, dry and refreshing with bright acidity. The most delicate style of Lambrusco.","producer_note":"Founded in 1920, Paltrinieri farms 17 hectares between the Secchia and Panaro rivers in Modena; 15 of them are the sandy-loam 'Cristo' vineyard where these grapes grow. The wine is named for St. Agatha, patron saint of Sorbara. Alberto Paltrinieri, who took over in 1998, was among the first to gamble on a mono-varietal Sorbara. (Research puts it at ~11% ABV and notes some bottlings blend in Salomino rather than being 100% Sorbara — confirm the vintage and blend with your lead before quoting.)","winemaking":"Grapes are gently pressed and vatted for a first fermentation, then a second fermentation by the Martinotti (Charmat) method in pressurized autoclaves, using native yeasts. Bottled at low pressure to preserve the fruit and varietal aromatics.","pron":[{"term":"Paltrinieri","pron":"pal-tree-nee-EH-ree"},{"term":"Sant'Agata","pron":"sahnt-AH-gah-tah"},{"term":"Lambrusco di Sorbara","pron":"lahm-BROOS-koh dee sor-BAH-rah"}],"specs":[{"label":"Body","rating":2,"low":"light","high":"full"},{"label":"Dryness","rating":4,"low":"sweet","high":"bone dry"},{"label":"Acidity","rating":4,"low":"soft","high":"bright"},{"label":"Tannins","rating":1,"low":"none","high":"grippy"}]},{"id":"wine:louis-armand-premier-cru-champagne","name":"Louis Armand Premier Cru Champagne","section":"Bubbles","type":"sparkling","region":"Champagne","country":"France","grapes":["Pinot Noir","Chardonnay","Pinot Meunier"],"vintage":null,"glass":25,"bottle":100,"status":"core-glass","vendor":"Vinea","cost":24.99,"cost_markable":true,"cost_note":null,"bottle_cost_pct":25.0,"glass_cost_pct":25.0,"glass_cost_pct_at_5":20.0,"pour":"Crisp and precise — citrus zest, white peach, and brioche with fine persistent bubbles and a clean, toasty finish. A polished Premier Cru Brut from Mareuil-sur-Aÿ.","producer_note":"Maison Louis Armand is a family-owned estate in Mareuil-sur-Aÿ, established in 1996. They supplement their own Premier Cru farming with fruit from neighboring 1ère Cru sites. Average vine age is 25 years on calcareous soils.","winemaking":"Hand-harvested grapes. Primary fermentation in stainless steel at controlled temperature. Traditional Champagne method (second fermentation in bottle); aged 3 years on lees. Dosage 8 g/l.","pron":[{"term":"Louis Armand","pron":"lwee ar-MON"},{"term":"Premier Cru","pron":"pruh-MYAY kroo"},{"term":"Mareuil-sur-Aÿ","pron":"mah-ROY soor ah-EE"},{"term":"Pinot Meunier","pron":"pee-noh muh-NYAY"}],"specs":[{"label":"Body","rating":2,"low":"light","high":"full"},{"label":"Dryness","rating":4,"low":"sweet","high":"bone dry"},{"label":"Acidity","rating":4,"low":"soft","high":"bright"},{"label":"Tannins","rating":1,"low":"none","high":"grippy"}]},{"id":"wine:muntanera--plessi--rosato","name":"Muntanera \"Plessi\" Rosato","section":"Bubbles","type":"sparkling","region":"Emilia-Romagna","country":"Italy","grapes":["Uva Tosca"],"vintage":2024,"glass":null,"bottle":70,"status":"phase-out","vendor":"Petit Monde","cost":17.0,"cost_markable":true,"cost_note":null,"bottle_cost_pct":24.3,"pour":"An earthy, funky, cloudy pink fizz with real tannic grip — cherry, wild berries, dusty roses, sage and a hint of pine. Ancestral method with no added sulfur; serve cold but not icy and let it open with air.","producer_note":"Claudio Plessi farms near Modena in Emilia-Romagna, where his parents planted vines in 1958. A professor of agriculture, he took over the estate in 1986 and founded Il Salto, a consortium of biodynamic growers and natural winemakers. Uva Tosca is a rare, hyper-local variety — reputedly the only grape able to ripen at these elevations — grown on super-calcareous silty clay-loam that was a riverbed a thousand years ago. 12% ABV.","winemaking":"Metodo ancestrale (pét-nat): a few hours of skin contact, then bottle-fermented and capped off without disgorging (sboccatura), per local tradition. Biodynamic farming, no added SO2, unfiltered and cloudy.","pron":[{"term":"Claudio Plessi","pron":"KLOW-dyoh PLESS-ee"},{"term":"Muntanera","pron":"moon-tah-NEH-rah"},{"term":"Uva Tosca","pron":"OO-vah TOS-kah"},{"term":"metodo ancestrale","pron":"MEH-toh-doh ahn-chess-TRAH-leh"}],"specs":[{"label":"Body","rating":3,"low":"light","high":"full"},{"label":"Dryness","rating":4,"low":"sweet","high":"bone dry"},{"label":"Acidity","rating":4,"low":"soft","high":"bright"},{"label":"Tannins","rating":3,"low":"none","high":"grippy"}]},{"id":"wine:laurent-perrier-champagne","name":"Laurent Perrier Champagne","section":"Bubbles","type":"sparkling","region":"Champagne","country":"France","grapes":["Chardonnay","Pinot Noir","Pinot Meunier"],"vintage":null,"glass":null,"bottle":125,"status":"core-bottle","vendor":"Winebow","cost":null,"cost_markable":false,"cost_note":null,"pour":"Elegant and Chardonnay-driven — delicate citrus blossom, vine peach, and white fruit on the nose, with beautiful freshness and a long, refined finish. Blended from over 100 reserve vintages.","producer_note":"Laurent-Perrier is based in Tours-sur-Marne, Champagne. Founded in 1812, the house is known for its Chardonnay-forward style and long cellar aging. La Cuvée is their flagship non-vintage blend, assembled from over 100 different wines and 100+ vintages of reserve wines.","winemaking":"Multi-vintage blend of more than 100 wines. Aged approximately 4 years in the cellar on lees before disgorgement. Traditional Champagne method.","pron":[{"term":"Laurent-Perrier","pron":"loh-RON peh-RYAY"},{"term":"Tours-sur-Marne","pron":"toor soor MARN"},{"term":"La Cuvée","pron":"lah koo-VAY"}],"specs":[{"label":"Body","rating":2,"low":"light","high":"full"},{"label":"Dryness","rating":4,"low":"sweet","high":"bone dry"},{"label":"Acidity","rating":4,"low":"soft","high":"bright"},{"label":"Tannins","rating":1,"low":"none","high":"grippy"}]},{"id":"wine:house-white--brigaldara-soave-doc","name":"House White: Brigaldara Soave DOC","section":"White/Rosé/Orange","type":"white","region":"Soave, Veneto","country":"Italy","grapes":["Garganega"],"vintage":2023,"glass":13,"bottle":50,"status":"core-glass","vendor":null,"cost":11.0,"cost_markable":false,"cost_note":"The $11.00 on the sheet is for the PREVIOUS house white (Le Vaglie Verdicchio). The house white is now the Brigaldara Soave and its cost is not on file. Unmarkable until Adrian confirms."},{"id":"wine:borgo-savaian-masaret--skins-are-kings--orange-nv","name":"Borgo Savaian Masaret \"Skins are Kings\" Orange NV","section":"White/Rosé/Orange","type":"orange","region":"Cormons, Friuli","country":"Italy","grapes":["Tocai Friulano","Ribolla Gialla","Istrian Malvasia"],"vintage":null,"glass":14,"bottle":55,"status":"core-glass","vendor":"Vinea","cost":13.99,"cost_markable":true,"cost_note":null,"bottle_cost_pct":25.4,"glass_cost_pct":25.0,"glass_cost_pct_at_5":20.0,"pour":"Bruised peach, tea, and cashews — a skin-contact Friulian field blend from a three-generation family estate 12km from the Slovenian border, unfiltered and earthy.","producer_note":"With roots stretching back three generations in Cormons, the Savaian family farms 18 hectares alongside Mount Quarin, maintaining a human-scale estate committed to indigenous varieties and natural farming.","winemaking":"Destemmed grapes fermented naturally on the skins in stainless steel at ~24°C. Post-fermentation skin maceration of approximately 90 days. Unfiltered, racked twice before bottling.","pron":[{"term":"Borgo Savaian","pron":"BOR-goh sah-vah-YAHN"},{"term":"Tocai Friulano","pron":"toh-KAH-ee free-oo-LAH-noh"},{"term":"Ribolla Gialla","pron":"ree-BOHL-lah JAHL-lah"},{"term":"Cormons","pron":"KOR-mohns"}],"specs":[{"label":"Body","rating":4,"low":"light","high":"full"},{"label":"Dryness","rating":5,"low":"sweet","high":"bone dry"},{"label":"Acidity","rating":3,"low":"soft","high":"bright"},{"label":"Tannins","rating":3,"low":"none","high":"grippy"}]},{"id":"wine:angelo-negro-vino-bianco--unfiltered-arneis","name":"Angelo Negro Vino Bianco, Unfiltered Arneis","section":"White/Rosé/Orange","type":"white","region":"Piedmont","country":"Italy","grapes":["Arneis"],"vintage":2024,"glass":15,"bottle":60,"status":"core-glass","vendor":"Vehrs","cost":14.69,"cost_markable":true,"cost_note":null,"bottle_cost_pct":24.5,"glass_cost_pct":24.5,"glass_cost_pct_at_5":19.6,"pour":"Tangerine, ripe pear, and lychee — unfiltered and unfined, so it carries a pleasing textural weight and mineral, slightly saline finish. A more expressive, natural take on Piedmont's signature white grape.","producer_note":"Angelo Negro & Figli has farmed in the Roero hills of Monteu Roero since 1670, making them one of the region's defining estates. Patriarch Giovanni Negro vinified the first dry Roero Arneis on record in 1971. Current winemaker Angelo Negro works exclusively with indigenous varieties across 70 organically farmed hectares on sandy, fossil-laden soils.","winemaking":"Hand-harvested Arneis, cryo-macerated for 8–10 hours, then fermented with native yeasts in stainless steel. Matured for 4+ months on the lees with regular bâtonnage. Bottled unfiltered and unfined.","pron":[{"term":"Angelo Negro","pron":"AHN-jeh-loh NEH-groh"},{"term":"Arneis","pron":"ar-NAY-ss"},{"term":"Roero","pron":"roh-EH-roh"},{"term":"bâtonnage","pron":"bah-toh-NAHJ"}],"specs":[{"label":"Body","rating":2,"low":"light","high":"full"},{"label":"Dryness","rating":5,"low":"sweet","high":"bone dry"},{"label":"Acidity","rating":4,"low":"soft","high":"bright"},{"label":"Tannins","rating":1,"low":"none","high":"grippy"}]},{"id":"wine:vigna-del-lauro-sauvignon-blanc--collio-doc","name":"Vigna del Lauro Sauvignon Blanc, Collio DOC","section":"White/Rosé/Orange","type":"white","region":"Collio, Friuli-Venezia Giulia","country":"Italy","grapes":["Sauvignon Blanc"],"vintage":2024,"glass":16,"bottle":65,"status":"core-glass","vendor":"Elliot Bay","cost":null,"cost_markable":false,"cost_note":null,"pour":"An intense, aromatic Collio Sauvignon off the ponca hills of Cormons — passion fruit and melon over sage, cut grass, and white pepper, with a savory, saline mineral edge. Stainless steel, aged on the fine lees; crisp and persistent on the finish.","producer_note":"Vigna del Lauro is a family-run Collio project rooted in Cormòns, farming the region's distinctive 'ponca' marl-and-sandstone soils in a savory, Italian counter-style to French Sancerre. Around 13% ABV per importer references — confirm with your lead before quoting.","winemaking":"100% Sauvignon Blanc, fermented entirely in stainless steel and aged on its fine lees; no oak.","pron":[{"term":"Vigna del Lauro","pron":"VEEN-yah del LOW-roh"},{"term":"Collio","pron":"KOH-lyoh"},{"term":"Cormòns","pron":"KOR-mohns"},{"term":"ponca","pron":"POHN-kah"}],"specs":[{"label":"Body","rating":2,"low":"light","high":"full"},{"label":"Dryness","rating":4,"low":"sweet","high":"bone dry"},{"label":"Acidity","rating":4,"low":"soft","high":"bright"},{"label":"Tannins","rating":1,"low":"none","high":"grippy"}]},{"id":"wine:la-spinetta--il-rosé-di-casanova--igt","name":"La Spinetta \"Il Rosé di Casanova\" IGT","section":"White/Rosé/Orange","type":"rosé","region":"Tuscany","country":"Italy","grapes":["Sangiovese","Prugnolo Gentile"],"vintage":2025,"glass":16,"bottle":65,"status":"core-glass","vendor":"Elliot Bay","cost":13.75,"cost_markable":true,"cost_note":null,"bottle_cost_pct":21.2,"glass_cost_pct":21.5,"glass_cost_pct_at_5":17.2,"pour":"Inspired by Provençal rosés — floral and fruity with hints of citrus and Mediterranean scrub, wonderfully fresh and delicate on the palate with satisfying minerality and all-embracing acidity.","producer_note":"La Spinetta (the Rivetti family) produced this rosé from vineyards in the municipality of Terricciola (Casanova), on sandy soils rich with oceanic sediments. First vintage was 2012; average production of 120,000 bottles.","winemaking":"Destemming and soft pressing without overstressing the berries. Brief skin contact to obtain extremely balanced free-run juice. Alcoholic fermentation for ~10 days, followed by aging in stainless steel on lees for ~3 months.","pron":[{"term":"La Spinetta","pron":"lah spee-NET-tah"},{"term":"Sangiovese","pron":"san-joh-VEH-zeh"},{"term":"Prugnolo Gentile","pron":"proo-NYOH-loh jen-TEE-leh"},{"term":"Terricciola","pron":"teh-ree-CHOH-lah"}],"specs":[{"label":"Body","rating":2,"low":"light","high":"full"},{"label":"Dryness","rating":5,"low":"sweet","high":"bone dry"},{"label":"Acidity","rating":4,"low":"soft","high":"bright"},{"label":"Tannins","rating":1,"low":"none","high":"grippy"}]},{"id":"wine:rocca-del-principe-fiano-di-avellino-docg","name":"Rocca del Principe Fiano di Avellino DOCG","section":"White/Rosé/Orange","type":"white","region":"Lapio, Irpinia, Campania","country":"Italy","grapes":["Fiano"],"vintage":2025,"glass":17,"bottle":70,"status":"core-glass","vendor":"Elliot Bay","cost":16.8,"cost_markable":true,"cost_note":null,"bottle_cost_pct":24.0,"glass_cost_pct":24.7,"glass_cost_pct_at_5":19.8,"pour":"White peach, lemon, lime, and crushed stone on the nose, lifted by flowers and aromatic herbs. Pure, fresh, and juicy on the palate, finishing with outstanding length and great acidity — a textbook, mineral-driven Fiano di Avellino.","producer_note":"Rocca del Principe is a small family estate in Lapio, one of the most respected crus for Fiano di Avellino. Vines (10-25 years) sit at 500-600 meters on the Arianiello hillside, split between complex volcanic soils and calcareous clay. Around 13.5% ABV per the importer, Indigenous Selections — confirm with your lead before quoting.","winemaking":"100% Fiano, hand-harvested. Fermented in stainless steel with partial malolactic, then aged on the fine lees in stainless for 10 months, plus a minimum 3-4 months in bottle before release.","pron":[{"term":"Fiano di Avellino","pron":"fee-AH-noh dee ah-vel-LEE-noh"},{"term":"Rocca del Principe","pron":"ROH-kah del PREEN-chee-peh"},{"term":"Lapio","pron":"LAH-pyoh"},{"term":"Irpinia","pron":"eer-PEE-nyah"}],"specs":[{"label":"Body","rating":3,"low":"light","high":"full"},{"label":"Dryness","rating":4,"low":"sweet","high":"bone dry"},{"label":"Acidity","rating":5,"low":"soft","high":"bright"},{"label":"Tannins","rating":1,"low":"none","high":"grippy"}]},{"id":"wine:la-spinetta-vermentino-igt","name":"La Spinetta Vermentino IGT","section":"White/Rosé/Orange","type":"white","region":"Terricciola, Tuscany","country":"Italy","grapes":["Vermentino"],"vintage":2025,"glass":18,"bottle":75,"status":"core-glass","vendor":null,"cost":null,"cost_markable":false,"cost_note":null,"pour":"Savory, floral and aromatic: Mediterranean scrub, white flowers and bright citrus over a mineral core, with real energy and a fresh, salty finish.","producer_note":"La Spinetta (the Rivetti family) farms its Tuscan estate at Casanova, in Terricciola, on sandy soils of marine origin rich in oceanic sediments: the same site as our rosé. First vintage 2009; about 50,000 bottles a year. This is the Vermentino we pour at the farm dinners.","winemaking":"Hand-harvested in early to mid September from southeast-facing vines about 15 years old. Fermented with natural yeasts, then about 3 months on the lees in stainless steel. Around 13% ABV per the importer.","pron":[{"term":"La Spinetta","pron":"lah spee-NET-tah"},{"term":"Vermentino","pron":"vehr-men-TEE-noh"},{"term":"Terricciola","pron":"teh-ree-CHOH-lah"}],"specs":[{"label":"Body","rating":2,"low":"light","high":"full"},{"label":"Dryness","rating":4,"low":"sweet","high":"bone dry"},{"label":"Acidity","rating":4,"low":"soft","high":"bright"},{"label":"Tannins","rating":1,"low":"none","high":"grippy"}]},{"id":"wine:tenuta-delle-terre-nere-etna-bianco-doc","name":"Tenuta delle Terre Nere Etna Bianco DOC","section":"White/Rosé/Orange","type":"white","region":"Etna, Sicily","country":"Italy","grapes":["Carricante","Catarratto","Inzolia","Grecanico","Minnella"],"vintage":2024,"glass":null,"bottle":85,"status":"core-bottle","vendor":null,"cost":null,"cost_markable":false,"cost_note":null,"pour":"Fresh and floral with a streak of white pepper and an assertive, smoky minerality. Light-bodied, silky-textured, with zesty but integrated acidity. Organically grown on the volcanic northern slope of Etna. Drink now or hold.","producer_note":"Tenuta delle Terre Nere sits on the northern slope of Etna between 600 and 900 meters, with roughly 43 hectares of vineyards and 150 olive trees. Its crus tell the story of the mountain: the Calderara Cru (11 to 12 hectares, some of it pre-phylloxera, the rest 40 to 50 years old), the Guardiola Cru (2.1 hectares, almost entirely pre-phylloxera and the highest-altitude red-grape vineyards in Europe), and Feudo di Mezzo. 500,000 years of eruptions have left endlessly varied soils, mostly volcanic ash flecked with black pumice and rock. The high elevation drives dramatic day-to-night temperature swings, giving Etna's wines their elegance and lift, free of the heat and overripeness of typical southern bottlings.","winemaking":null,"pron":[{"term":"Terre Nere","pron":"TEH-reh NEH-reh"},{"term":"Carricante","pron":"kar-ree-KAHN-teh"},{"term":"Catarratto","pron":"kah-tah-RAH-toh"},{"term":"Inzolia","pron":"een-ZOH-lee-ah"},{"term":"Grecanico","pron":"greh-KAH-nee-koh"},{"term":"Minnella","pron":"mee-NEL-lah"}],"specs":[]},{"id":"wine:viberti--derthona--timorasso--colli-tortonesi-doc","name":"Viberti \"Derthona\" Timorasso, Colli Tortonesi DOC","section":"White/Rosé/Orange","type":"white","region":"Colli Tortonesi, Piedmont","country":"Italy","grapes":["Timorasso"],"vintage":2023,"glass":null,"bottle":95,"status":"phase-out","vendor":null,"cost":null,"cost_markable":false,"cost_note":null,"pour":"Straw yellow in youth, turning golden with age. Peach, acacia flower and hawthorn up front, with the hydrocarbon and mineral notes Timorasso is famous for emerging after a few years in bottle. High acidity and low pH keep it taut and long-lived, with no malolactic. Serve around 14°C, and don't be afraid to cellar it 15 years or more.","producer_note":"Viberti Giovanni farms 9.5 hectares in the upper part of Monleale, a medieval hill town central to the Timorasso revival of the 1980s led by Walter Massa. The vineyards sit at around 400 meters with east to southeast exposure on light, clay-limestone soils ideal for the grape. \"Derthona\" is the ancient Latin name for nearby Tortona, heart of the four valleys that have always grown this rare Piedmontese white, one of the oldest and among the few capable of great long-aging wines.","winemaking":"Grapes are harvested in mid-September from the upper part of Monleale at 350 meters and trucked refrigerated to the Barolo winery. Pneumatic pressing, cold settling, and fermentation at 12°C for 10 to 14 days, then storage on fine lees. Malolactic does not occur, blocked by the cold and the grape's naturally high acidity and low pH. The wine then matures on its lees for 10 to 12 months with weekly batonnage.","pron":[{"term":"Viberti","pron":"vee-BEHR-tee"},{"term":"Derthona","pron":"der-TOH-nah"},{"term":"Timorasso","pron":"tee-moh-RAH-soh"},{"term":"Colli Tortonesi","pron":"KOH-lee tor-toh-NEH-zee"},{"term":"Monleale","pron":"mohn-leh-AH-leh"},{"term":"batonnage","pron":"bah-toh-NAHZH"}],"specs":[]},{"id":"wine:terlan--terlaner-i--primo-grande-cuvée-doc","name":"Terlan \"Terlaner I\" Primo Grande Cuvée DOC","section":"White/Rosé/Orange","type":"white","region":"Alto Adige","country":"Italy","grapes":["Pinot Bianco","Chardonnay","Sauvignon Blanc"],"vintage":2021,"glass":null,"bottle":340,"status":"phase-out","vendor":null,"cost":null,"cost_markable":false,"cost_note":null,"pour":"Rich and complex — citrus fruit, white pepper, herbs, and a delicate smoky note on the nose; extraordinary structure and subtle texture on the palate with an extremely long mineral finish. The supreme expression of a unique terroir.","producer_note":"Kellerei Cantina Terlan (Terlano), Alto Adige. Only 3,190 bottles produced. This old cuvée dates to the winery's founding; Pinot Bianco provides freshness and acid structure, Chardonnay adds warmth and mellowness, Sauvignon contributes aromatic finesse.","winemaking":"Gentle whole-cluster pressing; natural must sedimentation. Slow fermentation in 12hl oak casks; malolactic fermentation and aging on lees for 12 months in large wooden barrels. Assembled May 2023, bottled August 2023.","pron":[{"term":"Terlan / Terlano","pron":"tehr-LAHN / tehr-LAH-noh"},{"term":"Terlaner","pron":"tehr-LAH-ner"},{"term":"Pinot Bianco","pron":"PEE-noh BYAHN-koh"},{"term":"Kellerei Cantina","pron":"keh-leh-RYE kahn-TEE-nah"}],"specs":[{"label":"Body","rating":4,"low":"light","high":"full"},{"label":"Dryness","rating":5,"low":"sweet","high":"bone dry"},{"label":"Acidity","rating":4,"low":"soft","high":"bright"},{"label":"Tannins","rating":1,"low":"none","high":"grippy"}]},{"id":"wine:house-red--podere-ruggeri-corsini--matot--langhe-rosso-doc","name":"House Red: Podere Ruggeri Corsini \"Matot\" Langhe Rosso DOC","section":"Red","type":"red","region":"Monforte d'Alba, Piedmont","country":"Italy","grapes":["Dolcetto","Nebbiolo","Barbera"],"vintage":null,"glass":13,"bottle":50,"status":"core-glass","vendor":"Casa Bruno","cost":11.9,"cost_markable":true,"cost_note":null,"bottle_cost_pct":23.8,"glass_cost_pct":22.9,"glass_cost_pct_at_5":18.3,"pour":"Deep ruby with violet hues. Black cherry, blackberry and currant on the nose with earth, tobacco and leather; well-structured, with lively acidity, moderate tannins, juicy dark fruit and a peppery, dry finish.","producer_note":"Podere Ruggeri Corsini is a family-run estate in Monforte d'Alba, inside the larger Barolo DOCG.","winemaking":null,"pron":[{"term":"Ruggeri Corsini","pron":"roo-JEH-ree kor-SEE-nee"},{"term":"Matot","pron":"mah-TOHT"},{"term":"Langhe","pron":"LAHN-geh"}],"specs":[]},{"id":"wine:copertino-riserva--negroamaro","name":"Copertino Riserva, Negroamaro","section":"Red","type":"red","region":"Puglia","country":"Italy","grapes":["Negroamaro","Malvasia Nera","Montepulciano"],"vintage":2017,"glass":null,"bottle":55,"status":"core-bottle","vendor":"Vehrs","cost":11.25,"cost_markable":true,"cost_note":null,"bottle_cost_pct":20.5,"pour":"Ruby red with garnet hints — intense aromas of blackberry, plum, Mediterranean scrub, ripe fruit, and leather; warm and generous on the palate with complex tones of almond and blackcurrant.","producer_note":"Cantina Sociale di Copertino was founded in 1935 by 36 vine growers in the Salento peninsula between the Ionian and Adriatic seas. Today the cooperative has 300 members farming ~300 hectares on characteristic red Apulian soils shared with century-old olive trees.","winemaking":null,"pron":[{"term":"Copertino","pron":"koh-pehr-TEE-noh"},{"term":"Negroamaro","pron":"neh-groh-ah-MAH-roh"},{"term":"Malvasia Nera","pron":"mal-VAH-zyah NEH-rah"},{"term":"Salento","pron":"sah-LEN-toh"}],"specs":[{"label":"Body","rating":4,"low":"light","high":"full"},{"label":"Dryness","rating":5,"low":"sweet","high":"bone dry"},{"label":"Acidity","rating":3,"low":"soft","high":"bright"},{"label":"Tannins","rating":3,"low":"none","high":"grippy"}]},{"id":"wine:brigaldara-valpolicella-doc","name":"Brigaldara Valpolicella DOC","section":"Red","type":"red","region":"Veneto","country":"Italy","grapes":["Corvina","Corvinone","Rondinella"],"vintage":2024,"glass":15,"bottle":60,"status":"core-glass","vendor":null,"cost":null,"cost_markable":false,"cost_note":null,"pour":"A classic Valpolicella: fresh raspberry and dried cranberry with thyme, sage and forest floor, leading to a lively palate driven by sour cherry. Generous, and it picks up spice and floral notes as it opens.","producer_note":"The Cesari family sold all their grapes until the 1980s; the first commercial bottling came in 1988 with Valpolicella legend Roberto Ferrarini. Stefano Cesari, once president of the Valpolicella producers' consortium, pushed for a cleaner, fresher, terroir-driven style. The estate farms sustainably across all three Valpolicella zones; this wine blends the Marcellise and Brigaldara vineyards.","winemaking":"Three weeks in stainless steel with délestage (a heavy pump-over that pulls fresh fruit rather than tannin), then 6 months in stainless steel.","pron":[{"term":"Brigaldara","pron":"bree-gahl-DAH-rah"},{"term":"Valpolicella","pron":"vahl-poh-lee-CHEL-lah"},{"term":"Corvinone","pron":"kor-vee-NOH-neh"},{"term":"délestage","pron":"day-less-TAHZH"}],"specs":[]},{"id":"wine:la-valentina-montepulciano-d-abruzzo--spelt--riserva","name":"La Valentina Montepulciano d'Abruzzo \"Spelt\" Riserva","section":"Red","type":"red","region":"Abruzzo","country":"Italy","grapes":["Montepulciano"],"vintage":2021,"glass":17,"bottle":70,"status":"core-glass","vendor":"Winebow","cost":14.89,"cost_markable":true,"cost_note":null,"bottle_cost_pct":21.3,"glass_cost_pct":21.9,"glass_cost_pct_at_5":17.5,"pour":"Bright cherry fruit on the nose, medium-bodied with vibrant acidity — intense black fruit, grainy velvety tannins, and a focused, long, balanced finish. Better after another year in the cellar.","producer_note":"La Valentina is based in Spoltore (Abruzzo), a village whose name derives from \"spelt\" — the same wheat that once grew where the winery now stands.","winemaking":null,"pron":[{"term":"La Valentina","pron":"lah vah-len-TEE-nah"},{"term":"Montepulciano d'Abruzzo","pron":"mon-teh-pool-CHAH-noh dah-BROOD-zoh"},{"term":"Riserva","pron":"ree-ZEHR-vah"}],"specs":[{"label":"Body","rating":3,"low":"light","high":"full"},{"label":"Dryness","rating":5,"low":"sweet","high":"bone dry"},{"label":"Acidity","rating":4,"low":"soft","high":"bright"},{"label":"Tannins","rating":3,"low":"none","high":"grippy"}]},{"id":"wine:g-d--vajra-langhe-nebbiolo-doc","name":"G.D. Vajra Langhe Nebbiolo DOC","section":"Red","type":"red","region":"Piedmont","country":"Italy","grapes":["Nebbiolo"],"vintage":2024,"glass":18,"bottle":75,"status":"core-glass","vendor":null,"cost":null,"cost_markable":false,"cost_note":null,"pour":"Lively garnet in the glass — wild strawberry, red cherry, and raspberry with a hint of goji berry, lifted by floral and gentle spice. Crunchy and vibrant on the palate, juicy red fruit woven through a well-defined tannic frame, finishing perfumed, floral, and gourmand.","producer_note":"G.D. Vajra, founded in 1972 by Aldo Vaira and named for his father Giuseppe Domenico Vajra, is one of Barolo's pioneering organic estates, based in Vergne — the highest village in the Barolo commune. This Langhe Nebbiolo is the estate's fresher, earlier-drinking expression of the grape, raised in steel rather than the long cask aging of their Barolo. Note: the fresh sheet header lists the 2024 vintage while the accompanying tasting and harvest notes describe the 2025 — confirm the vintage on the bottle with your lead before quoting. ABV runs about 13.5% per the producer; confirm before quoting.","winemaking":"100% Nebbiolo from organically farmed vineyards on calcareous soils between 280–550m, worked with permanent grass cover; the estate is certified organic and Equalitas sustainable. Hand-harvested over a late, extended pick (Sept 12–Oct 2 in the described vintage). Fermentation ran 20–25 days followed by spontaneous malolactic fermentation, then roughly 4 months in stainless steel before bottling. No oak — the freshness and lift come from steel-only aging.","pron":[{"term":"G.D. Vajra","pron":"VY-rah"},{"term":"Langhe","pron":"LAHN-gheh"},{"term":"Nebbiolo","pron":"neb-BYOH-loh"},{"term":"Piemonte","pron":"pyeh-MON-teh"}],"specs":[{"label":"Body","rating":3,"low":"light","high":"full"},{"label":"Dryness","rating":5,"low":"sweet","high":"bone dry"},{"label":"Acidity","rating":4,"low":"soft","high":"bright"},{"label":"Tannins","rating":3,"low":"none","high":"grippy"}]},{"id":"wine:corte-pavone-rosso-di-montalcino-doc","name":"Corte Pavone Rosso di Montalcino DOC","section":"Red","type":"red","region":"Montalcino, Tuscany","country":"Italy","grapes":["Sangiovese"],"vintage":2023,"glass":19,"bottle":80,"status":"core-glass","vendor":"Specialty Cellars","cost":19.83,"cost_markable":true,"cost_note":null,"bottle_cost_pct":24.8,"glass_cost_pct":26.1,"glass_cost_pct_at_5":20.9,"pour":"Packed with blackberry, black cherry, cedar, prune, and espresso — complex and spicy, with mouth-watering acidity, lively tannins, and a long, dry, persistent finish with lingering leather and tobacco.","producer_note":"Corte Pavone was converted to organic and biodynamic farming in the early 1970s by owner Rainer Loacker in the Casanuova area of Montalcino at 1,500 ft elevation. Winemaker Hayo Loacker ages in Slavonian, French, and Austrian oak. Certified vegan, organic, biodynamic, and La Renaissance des Appellations.","winemaking":"70% of the Sangiovese aged in used barrel, 30% in tonneau for 18 months. Placed in stainless steel tanks for 1 month before bottling.","pron":[{"term":"Corte Pavone","pron":"KOR-teh pah-VOH-neh"},{"term":"Rosso di Montalcino","pron":"ROHS-soh dee mon-tahl-CHEE-noh"},{"term":"Sangiovese","pron":"san-joh-VEH-zeh"}],"specs":[{"label":"Body","rating":3,"low":"light","high":"full"},{"label":"Dryness","rating":5,"low":"sweet","high":"bone dry"},{"label":"Acidity","rating":4,"low":"soft","high":"bright"},{"label":"Tannins","rating":3,"low":"none","high":"grippy"}]},{"id":"wine:podere-ruggeri-corsini--armujan--barbera-d-alba-superiore-do","name":"Podere Ruggeri Corsini \"Armujan\" Barbera d'Alba Superiore DOC","section":"Red","type":"red","region":"Monforte d'Alba, Piedmont","country":"Italy","grapes":["Barbera"],"vintage":2022,"glass":null,"bottle":80,"status":"core-bottle","vendor":"Casa Bruno","cost":19.6,"cost_markable":true,"cost_note":null,"bottle_cost_pct":24.5,"pour":"Cherry and blueberry pie, orange oil, baking spices, clay earth — unctuous and full on the palate with velvety tannins, jammy dark berries, and burnt orange peel carried by a subtle current of acidity that lands gracefully on the finish.","producer_note":"Podere Ruggeri Corsini is a family-run estate in Monforte d'Alba. The Armujan comes from 66–76-year-old vines on clayey calcareous soils, hand-picked.","winemaking":"Manual harvest in crates; immediate stalk removal; four pump-overs per day the first week. 12-day maceration. Malolactic fermentation, then 18 months in a combination of French barriques (different ages) and large Slavonian oak. Bottled July 2024, released after 3 months bottle age. Barbera d'Alba Superiore requires minimum 12 months aging, at least 4 in oak.","pron":[{"term":"Podere Ruggeri Corsini","pron":"poh-DEH-reh rood-JEH-ree kor-SEE-nee"},{"term":"Armujan","pron":"ar-moo-YAHN"},{"term":"Barbera d'Alba","pron":"bar-BEH-rah DAL-bah"},{"term":"Monforte d'Alba","pron":"mon-FOR-teh DAL-bah"}],"specs":[{"label":"Body","rating":4,"low":"light","high":"full"},{"label":"Dryness","rating":5,"low":"sweet","high":"bone dry"},{"label":"Acidity","rating":4,"low":"soft","high":"bright"},{"label":"Tannins","rating":2,"low":"none","high":"grippy"}]},{"id":"wine:felsina--berardenga--chianti-classico-docg","name":"Felsina \"Berardenga\" Chianti Classico DOCG","section":"Red","type":"red","region":"Chianti Classico, Tuscany","country":"Italy","grapes":["Sangiovese"],"vintage":2022,"glass":null,"bottle":90,"status":"core-bottle","vendor":"Elliot Bay","cost":21.7,"cost_markable":true,"cost_note":null,"bottle_cost_pct":24.1,"pour":"Ruby red with deep intensity — wild berries, spicy notes, fruity on the palate with good tannins and a lingering finish. Grown on the southern border of Chianti Classico zone.","producer_note":"Felsina's Berardenga estate sits northeast of Siena in the commune of Castelnuovo Berardenga. Vineyards were thoroughly researched in the 1970s–80s to identify the finest parent plants; replanted between 1990 and early 2000s.","winemaking":"Quality-selected clusters destemmed and pressed, fermented and macerated in stainless steel 12–15 days at 28–30°C with programmed punchdowns and daily pumpovers. New wine goes into medium Slavonian oak barrels in March–April, small percentage in twice/thrice used oak; 12 months maturation, then blended, bottled, and minimum 3 months bottle age.","pron":[{"term":"Felsina","pron":"fel-SEE-nah"},{"term":"Berardenga","pron":"beh-rar-DEN-gah"},{"term":"Chianti Classico","pron":"KYAHN-tee KLAHS-see-koh"},{"term":"Castelnuovo Berardenga","pron":"kah-stel-NWOH-voh beh-rar-DEN-gah"}],"specs":[{"label":"Body","rating":3,"low":"light","high":"full"},{"label":"Dryness","rating":5,"low":"sweet","high":"bone dry"},{"label":"Acidity","rating":4,"low":"soft","high":"bright"},{"label":"Tannins","rating":3,"low":"none","high":"grippy"}]},{"id":"wine:franco-molino--cascina-rocca--barolo-docg","name":"Franco Molino \"Cascina Rocca\" Barolo DOCG","section":"Red","type":"red","region":"Barolo, Piedmont","country":"Italy","grapes":["Nebbiolo"],"vintage":2020,"glass":null,"bottle":95,"status":"core-bottle","vendor":null,"cost":null,"cost_markable":false,"cost_note":null,"pour":"Bright garnet with brickly shades — delicate and sweet-spiced scent on the nose; pleasantly warm on the palate with well-balanced acids and tannins and a slight, elegant wood note from aging.","producer_note":"Franco Molino's Cascina Rocca Barolo is made from selected Nebbiolo from the Bricco Zuncai (Barolo) and Bricco Rocca (La Morra) vineyards, totaling 3.7 ha with south-west and southern exposures. Vines planted in 1960 and 1980.","winemaking":"Hand-harvested in small crates in early October, immediately pressed. Traditional fermentation with marc maceration for ~10 days in controlled-temperature steel tanks (26–28°C) with frequent soft pump-overs. Spontaneous malolactic fermentation. Ripened in large French oak casks (1,500–3,000L) for 24 months, then 12 months refinement in steel before bottling.","pron":[{"term":"Franco Molino","pron":"FRAHN-koh moh-LEE-noh"},{"term":"Cascina Rocca","pron":"kah-SHEE-nah ROHK-kah"},{"term":"Barolo","pron":"bah-ROH-loh"},{"term":"Nebbiolo","pron":"neb-BYOH-loh"}],"specs":[{"label":"Body","rating":4,"low":"light","high":"full"},{"label":"Dryness","rating":5,"low":"sweet","high":"bone dry"},{"label":"Acidity","rating":4,"low":"soft","high":"bright"},{"label":"Tannins","rating":4,"low":"none","high":"grippy"}]},{"id":"wine:piaggia--il-sasso--carmignano-docg","name":"Piaggia \"Il Sasso\" Carmignano DOCG","section":"Red","type":"red","region":"Carmignano, Tuscany","country":"Italy","grapes":["Sangiovese","Cabernet Sauvignon","Merlot","Cabernet Franc"],"vintage":2022,"glass":null,"bottle":100,"status":"core-bottle","vendor":null,"cost":null,"cost_markable":false,"cost_note":null,"pour":"Deep, intense ruby red. Cherry and marasca cherry lifted by balsamic and spice, then full and enveloping on the palate with the polish of thoroughbred Sangiovese — fine-grained tannins framing bright, well-balanced acidity, the Cabernet and Merlot rounding the edges. A structured Carmignano for the richer plates on the menu.","producer_note":"Piaggia is a small family estate (the Vannucci family) in Carmignano, one of Tuscany's oldest and smallest DOCG zones, where Sangiovese has long been blended with a meaningful share of Cabernet. \"Il Sasso\" is the estate's Sangiovese-led blend, farmed practicing-organic, with roughly 3,300 cases produced and about 1,000 imported. The fresh sheet doesn't list a vintage — confirm the pour vintage with your lead before quoting.","winemaking":"Fermented with ambient (indigenous) yeasts, with maceration of at least 18–20 days. Aged at least 15 months in French-oak barriques, then at least 6 months in bottle before release.","pron":[{"term":"Piaggia","pron":"PYAH-jah"},{"term":"Il Sasso","pron":"eel SAH-soh"},{"term":"Carmignano","pron":"kar-mee-NYAH-noh"},{"term":"marasca","pron":"mah-RAH-skah"}],"specs":[{"label":"Body","rating":4,"low":"light","high":"full"},{"label":"Dryness","rating":5,"low":"sweet","high":"bone dry"},{"label":"Acidity","rating":4,"low":"soft","high":"bright"},{"label":"Tannins","rating":4,"low":"none","high":"grippy"}]},{"id":"wine:tenuta-delle-terre-nere--bellacolonna--etna-rosso-doc","name":"Tenuta delle Terre Nere \"Bellacolonna\" Etna Rosso DOC","section":"Red","type":"red","region":"Etna, Sicily","country":"Italy","grapes":["Nerello Mascalese"],"vintage":2022,"glass":null,"bottle":100,"status":"core-bottle","vendor":null,"cost":null,"cost_markable":false,"cost_note":null,"pour":"Autumnal spices, dusty rose, blood orange, and dried strawberries — crunchy wild berry fruits and mineral tones on the palate, driven by zesty acidity, with silky tannins and a violet-tinged, confectionery-spiced finale.","producer_note":"Terre Nere farms roughly 43 hectares on the northern slopes of Mount Etna, including the Calderara Sottana contrada. Soils are extremely rocky volcanic ash, black pumice, and solid volcanic rock. The estate includes pre-phylloxera vines and hosts the highest-altitude red-grape vineyards in Europe (Guardiola Cru). Harvest typically occurs in the last weeks of October.","winemaking":"Fermentation under controlled temperatures with spontaneous malolactic fermentation. Aged in French oak barriques, tonneaux, and cask (10% new oak) for 14 months, then rested in stainless steel before bottling.","pron":[{"term":"Tenuta delle Terre Nere","pron":"teh-NOO-tah DEL-leh TER-reh NEH-reh"},{"term":"Bellacolonna","pron":"bel-lah-koh-LON-nah"},{"term":"Nerello Mascalese","pron":"neh-REL-loh mah-skah-LEH-zeh"},{"term":"contrada","pron":"kon-TRAH-dah"}],"specs":[{"label":"Body","rating":2,"low":"light","high":"full"},{"label":"Dryness","rating":5,"low":"sweet","high":"bone dry"},{"label":"Acidity","rating":5,"low":"soft","high":"bright"},{"label":"Tannins","rating":3,"low":"none","high":"grippy"}]},{"id":"wine:castello-di-neive-barbaresco-docg","name":"Castello di Neive Barbaresco DOCG","section":"Red","type":"red","region":"Barbaresco, Piedmont","country":"Italy","grapes":["Nebbiolo"],"vintage":2023,"glass":null,"bottle":135,"status":"core-bottle","vendor":"Winebow","cost":30.0,"cost_markable":false,"cost_note":"Sheet cost is for Santo Stefano 2022; the menu prints a straight Barbaresco DOCG 2023.","pour":"Crushed flowers, bright red fruit, tobacco, cedar, and mint — medium-weight with blood orange, raspberry, and dried roses arching across the palate. Classy, pretty, and absolutely stellar in its peer group.","producer_note":"Castello di Neive draws from some of the most respected vineyards in Barbaresco DOCG: Gallina, Messoirano, Valtorta, and a portion of Santo Stefano. Vines average 30 years of age.","winemaking":"Grapes pressed, then matured in wooden vats for 2 years to smooth tannins and develop complex flavors.","pron":[{"term":"Castello di Neive","pron":"kah-STEL-loh dee NAY-veh"},{"term":"Barbaresco","pron":"bar-bah-RES-koh"},{"term":"Nebbiolo","pron":"neb-BYOH-loh"},{"term":"Gallina","pron":"gahl-LEE-nah"}],"specs":[{"label":"Body","rating":3,"low":"light","high":"full"},{"label":"Dryness","rating":5,"low":"sweet","high":"bone dry"},{"label":"Acidity","rating":4,"low":"soft","high":"bright"},{"label":"Tannins","rating":4,"low":"none","high":"grippy"}]},{"id":"wine:voliero-brunello-di-montalcino-docg","name":"Voliero Brunello di Montalcino DOCG","section":"Red","type":"red","region":"Montalcino, Tuscany","country":"Italy","grapes":["Sangiovese"],"vintage":2017,"glass":null,"bottle":145,"status":"core-bottle","vendor":"Elliot Bay","cost":49.0,"cost_markable":true,"cost_note":null,"bottle_cost_pct":33.8,"pour":"Elegant and high-altitude Brunello — bright cherry, dried roses, tobacco, and a mineral lift from 350m elevation vines. More perfumed and finessed than a classic Montalcino, with great aging ahead.","producer_note":"Voliero is the second label of Andrea Cortonesi (of Uccelliera), born in 2006 to showcase fruit from high-altitude vineyards in Castelnuovo dell'Abate and Sant'Angelo in Colle — 200 meters higher than the Uccelliera estate. Production is tiny: roughly 600 cases of Brunello per year. Cortonesi describes Voliero as expressing the elegance and brightness that elevation imparts, versus Uccelliera's power and depth.","winemaking":"100% Sangiovese from 15–25-year-old vines on clay soils at high elevation. Cold maceration with skins at 10–12°C followed by spontaneous fermentation in temperature-controlled stainless steel for 20–25 days. Malolactic fermentation without added bacteria. Aged 30 months in large French and Slavonian oak casks (20, 30 & 40 hl).","pron":[{"term":"Voliero","pron":"voh-LYEH-roh"},{"term":"Brunello di Montalcino","pron":"broo-NEL-loh dee mon-tahl-CHEE-noh"},{"term":"Castelnuovo dell'Abate","pron":"kah-stel-NWOH-voh del-AH-bah-teh"}],"specs":[{"label":"Body","rating":4,"low":"light","high":"full"},{"label":"Dryness","rating":5,"low":"sweet","high":"bone dry"},{"label":"Acidity","rating":4,"low":"soft","high":"bright"},{"label":"Tannins","rating":4,"low":"none","high":"grippy"}]},{"id":"wine:cocito--baluchin--barbaresco-riserva-docg","name":"Cocito \"Baluchin\" Barbaresco Riserva DOCG","section":"Red","type":"red","region":"Barbaresco, Piedmont","country":"Italy","grapes":["Nebbiolo"],"vintage":2017,"glass":null,"bottle":165,"status":"phase-out","vendor":"Elliot Bay","cost":56.0,"cost_markable":true,"cost_note":null,"bottle_cost_pct":33.9,"pour":"Crushed flowers, sweet red cherry, cinnamon, white pepper, cedar, and a touch of French oak, with violets, flint, and dark cassis and plum underneath. Translucent, high-altitude Barbaresco — super-expressive and impeccably done. 94 points, Vinous.","producer_note":"Ezio Cocito (pronounced Co-CHEE-toh) farms a tiny 1.2-hectare, high-elevation vineyard in Neive, one of the highest sites in the Barbaresco appellation at ~380 meters with a steep 45-55% south/southwest slope and 30+ year-old vines. Made in collaboration with, and aged at, Giorgio Rivetti's La Spinetta; only 300-400 six-pack cases a year, and Riserva only in exceptional vintages. Around 14.5% ABV per the importer, Indigenous Selections — confirm with your lead before quoting.","winemaking":"100% Nebbiolo, aged roughly 20 months in 30% new French oak. Unfined and unfiltered.","pron":[{"term":"Cocito","pron":"co-CHEE-toh"},{"term":"Baluchin","pron":"bah-loo-KEEN"},{"term":"Barbaresco","pron":"bar-bah-RES-koh"},{"term":"Nebbiolo","pron":"neb-BYOH-loh"},{"term":"Neive","pron":"NAY-veh"}],"specs":[{"label":"Body","rating":4,"low":"light","high":"full"},{"label":"Dryness","rating":5,"low":"sweet","high":"bone dry"},{"label":"Acidity","rating":4,"low":"soft","high":"bright"},{"label":"Tannins","rating":4,"low":"none","high":"grippy"}]},{"id":"wine:cantina-terlano--porphyr--lagrein-riserva-doc","name":"Cantina Terlano \"Porphyr\" Lagrein Riserva DOC","section":"Red","type":"red","region":"Alto Adige","country":"Italy","grapes":["Lagrein"],"vintage":2021,"glass":null,"bottle":190,"status":"phase-out","vendor":null,"cost":null,"cost_markable":false,"cost_note":null,"pour":"Morello cherry and bilberry with licorice, coffee, vanilla, clove, and black tea on the nose — harmonious and multi-layered on the palate, juicy fruit with spice and a slightly sweet dark chocolate note rounded off by silky tannins.","producer_note":"Cantina Terlano (Alto Adige) produces this flagship Lagrein Riserva, Porphyr — named for the porphyry volcanic rock of the region.","winemaking":"Manual harvest and selection; destemming followed by slow must fermentation at controlled temperature with gentle agitation in stainless steel. Malolactic fermentation, then 18 months aging in barriques (one third new). Blended three months before bottling.","pron":[{"term":"Cantina Terlano","pron":"kahn-TEE-nah tehr-LAH-noh"},{"term":"Porphyr","pron":"POR-feer"},{"term":"Lagrein","pron":"lah-GRYNE"}],"specs":[{"label":"Body","rating":5,"low":"light","high":"full"},{"label":"Dryness","rating":5,"low":"sweet","high":"bone dry"},{"label":"Acidity","rating":3,"low":"soft","high":"bright"},{"label":"Tannins","rating":4,"low":"none","high":"grippy"}]},{"id":"wine:tenuta-santa-maria-amarone-della-valpolicella-classico-riser","name":"Tenuta Santa Maria Amarone della Valpolicella Classico Riserva DOCG","section":"Red","type":"red","region":"Valpolicella Classico, Veneto","country":"Italy","grapes":["Corvina","Corvinone","Rondinella"],"vintage":2018,"glass":null,"bottle":200,"status":"phase-out","vendor":"Elliot Bay","cost":67.0,"cost_markable":true,"cost_note":null,"bottle_cost_pct":33.5,"pour":"Preserved cherry, spices, and dried rose petals on the nose — silky and cooling on the palate with dark red fruits, fine tannins, dark chocolate, and blood orange through a dramatically long, classically structured finale.","producer_note":"Tenuta Santa Maria's vineyards are in the Negrar di Valpolicella valley on marine-origin soils of limestone, fossils, and clay. Upper vineyards are terraced with south-east exposure.","winemaking":"Traditional appassimento method: hand-harvested Corvina, Corvinone, and Rondinella dried naturally, then long fermentation in stainless steel. Amarone aged 5 years in large oak barrels.","pron":[{"term":"Amarone della Valpolicella","pron":"ah-mah-ROH-neh DEL-lah val-poh-lee-CHEL-lah"},{"term":"Corvinone","pron":"kor-vee-NOH-neh"},{"term":"appassimento","pron":"ah-pahs-see-MEN-toh"}],"specs":[{"label":"Body","rating":5,"low":"light","high":"full"},{"label":"Dryness","rating":5,"low":"sweet","high":"bone dry"},{"label":"Acidity","rating":3,"low":"soft","high":"bright"},{"label":"Tannins","rating":4,"low":"none","high":"grippy"}]},{"id":"wine:viberti-giovanni--san-pietro--barolo-riserva-docg","name":"Viberti Giovanni \"San Pietro\" Barolo Riserva DOCG","section":"Red","type":"red","region":"Barolo, Piedmont","country":"Italy","grapes":["Nebbiolo"],"vintage":2018,"glass":null,"bottle":240,"status":"phase-out","vendor":"Elliot Bay","cost":96.0,"cost_markable":true,"cost_note":null,"bottle_cost_pct":40.0,"pour":"Balsam and dry tobacco on the nose with pressed roses — iron and raw meat with fine mineral quality on the palate, juicy and bright with chalky tannins, cranberry and red cherry, layered and pure with classic Barolo austerity.","producer_note":"Viberti Giovanni is based in the village of Barolo, Piedmont.","winemaking":null,"pron":[{"term":"Viberti Giovanni","pron":"vee-BEHR-tee joh-VAHN-nee"},{"term":"Barolo Riserva","pron":"bah-ROH-loh ree-ZEHR-vah"},{"term":"Nebbiolo","pron":"neb-BYOH-loh"}],"specs":[{"label":"Body","rating":4,"low":"light","high":"full"},{"label":"Dryness","rating":5,"low":"sweet","high":"bone dry"},{"label":"Acidity","rating":4,"low":"soft","high":"bright"},{"label":"Tannins","rating":5,"low":"none","high":"grippy"}]}],"distractors":[{"name":"Le Origini \"Passanello\" Lambrusco Grasparossa 2024","type":"Sparkling","never_ask":true},{"name":"House White: Le Vaglie Verdicchio","type":"White","never_ask":true},{"name":"Valle dell'Acate \"Thymbra\" Vermentino 2023","type":"White","never_ask":true},{"name":"Corte Scaletta Valpolicella 2022","type":"Red","never_ask":true},{"name":"Domaine Lingot-Martin Pet Nat Rosé","type":"Sparkling","never_ask":true},{"name":"Il Ceo \"Vespri\" Pet Nat","type":"Sparkling","never_ask":true},{"name":"Pojer e Sandri \"Zero Infinito\" Pet Nat","type":"Sparkling","never_ask":true},{"name":"Folicello \"Il Rosso\" Lambrusco dell'Emilia","type":"Sparkling","never_ask":true},{"name":"Apres Sol Brut Rosé","type":"Sparkling","never_ask":true},{"name":"Pietramore Trebbiano d'Abruzzo Superiore 2021","type":"White","never_ask":true},{"name":"Pietramore Passerina Abruzzo Superiore","type":"White","never_ask":true},{"name":"I Pentri \"Flora\" Falanghina 2021","type":"White","never_ask":true},{"name":"L'Archetipo Greco Bianco 2024","type":"White","never_ask":true},{"name":"Scarpa Monferrato Bianco (Timorasso) 2020","type":"White","never_ask":true},{"name":"Fontodi \"Meriggio\" Bianco 2022","type":"White","never_ask":true},{"name":"Gilbert Cellars Orange 2024","type":"Orange","never_ask":true},{"name":"Ruth Lewandowski \"Tatto\" Orange 2022","type":"Orange","never_ask":true},{"name":"Le Vigne di Faraone Cerasuolo d'Abruzzo 2024","type":"Rosé","never_ask":true},{"name":"Boffalora \"Umo\" Chiavennasca 2021","type":"Red","never_ask":true},{"name":"I Pentri \"Kerres\" Piedirosso 2016","type":"Red","never_ask":true},{"name":"La Miraja Ruché di Castagnole Monferrato 2023","type":"Red","never_ask":true},{"name":"Uccelliera \"Rapace\" 2019","type":"Red","never_ask":true},{"name":"Scarpa Verduno Pelaverga 2022","type":"Red","never_ask":true},{"name":"Zenato \"Cresasso\" Corvina 2015","type":"Red","never_ask":true},{"name":"Arpepe \"Sassella Rocce Rosse\" Riserva","type":"Red","never_ask":true},{"name":"Vietti Barolo \"Ravera\" 2020","type":"Red","never_ask":true},{"name":"Antinori \"Tignanello\" 2021","type":"Red","never_ask":true}],"after_dinner":[{"id":"ad:espresso-martini","name":"Espresso Martini","price":"16","note":"Vodka, espresso, coffee liqueur — cold, rich, caffeinated closer.","group":"Digestivi & liqueurs"},{"id":"ad:house-limoncello","name":"House Limoncello","price":"12","note":"House-made lemon liqueur; bright, sweet, clean finish. Made in-house.","group":"Digestivi & liqueurs"},{"id":"ad:scuppoz-amaretto","name":"Scuppoz Amaretto","price":"12","note":"Toasted almond and apricot kernel amaretto from Abruzzo, Italy; marzipan sweetness, nutty depth.","group":"Digestivi & liqueurs"},{"id":"ad:scuppoz-amaro","name":"Scuppoz Amaro","price":"12","note":"Abruzzo, Italy (Scuppoz); artisanal mountain-herb amaro, made without added colorants.","group":"Amari"},{"id":"ad:sambuca","name":"Sambuca","price":"8","note":"Italian anise liqueur; sweet, licorice-forward, traditionally served with three coffee beans.","group":"Digestivi & liqueurs"},{"id":"ad:dow-s-tawny-porto-20-yr","name":"Dow's Tawny Porto 20 yr","price":"15","note":"Aged 20 years in cask; Douro Valley, Portugal. Nutty, dried fig, candied orange, warm spice.","group":"Port, sherry & vermouth"},{"id":"ad:dow-s-fine-white-port","name":"Dow's Fine White Port","price":"9","note":"Fresh, golden white port; Douro Valley, Portugal. Crisp pear and citrus, nutty finish, slightly off-dry.","group":"Port, sherry & vermouth"},{"id":"ad:mancino-edizione-sakura-vermouth","name":"Mancino Edizione Sakura Vermouth","price":"14","note":"Limited-edition Torino-style vermouth; Kyoto cherry blossoms and Italian viola flowers, pale pink, delicately floral.","group":"Port, sherry & vermouth"},{"id":"ad:lustau-oloroso-sherry","name":"Lustau Oloroso Sherry","price":"10","note":"Fully oxidized dry sherry from Jerez, Spain. Dark, full-bodied; caramel, walnut, dried fruit, warm spice.","group":"Port, sherry & vermouth"},{"id":"ad:lustau-pedro-ximenez-sherry","name":"Lustau Pedro-Ximenez Sherry","price":"10","note":"Lusciously sweet sherry from Jerez, Spain. Sun-dried PX grapes; raisins, molasses, dark fig, velvety texture.","group":"Port, sherry & vermouth"},{"id":"ad:amaricano-bianco","name":"Amaricano Bianco","price":"11","note":"Seattle-made (Fast Penny Spirits); citrus-forward American bitter aperitivo. Candied lemon, saffron, apricot, chamomile.","group":"Amari"},{"id":"ad:averna","name":"Averna","price":"9","note":"Sicily, Italy (since 1868); bittersweet and caramel-rich, with orange peel and licorice. Smooth, approachable, a good first amaro.","group":"Amari"},{"id":"ad:scarlet-orange-amaro","name":"Scarlet Orange Amaro","price":"16","note":"Japanese craft amaro by Iseya Distillery, Kanagawa. Blood orange-forward; bright citrus, lemon balm, gentle ginger heat.","group":"Amari"},{"id":"ad:scarlet-fernet-amaro","name":"Scarlet Fernet Amaro","price":"14","note":"Japanese craft fernet by Iseya Distillery; 30+ botanicals including mint, galangal, and palo santo. Botanical and layered, surprisingly smooth.","group":"Fernet"},{"id":"ad:amaro-foro-originale","name":"Amaro Foro Originale","price":"12","note":"Piedmontese amaro; 25 botanicals including gentian and angelica. Bittersweet citrus, dried herbs, clean bitter finish.","group":"Amari"},{"id":"ad:faccia-brutto-fernet-pianta","name":"Faccia Brutto Fernet Pianta","price":"14","note":"Brooklyn-made (Faccia Brutto Spirits); 27 botanicals, mint-forward with citrus peel and licorice. More approachable than traditional fernet.","group":"Fernet"},{"id":"ad:faccia-brutto-amaro-gorini","name":"Faccia Brutto Amaro Gorini","price":"14","note":"Brooklyn-made (Faccia Brutto Spirits); barrel-aged in Solera style. Orange peel, chestnut honey, warm cinnamon and star anise.","group":"Amari"},{"id":"ad:fernet-dogma","name":"Fernet Dogma","price":"9","note":"Chicago-made (CH Distillery); fernet with Dark Matter coffee and chamomile, finished in bourbon barrels. Coffee, spearmint, slight bitterness.","group":"Fernet"},{"id":"ad:fernet-francisco-rye-cask","name":"Fernet Francisco Rye Cask","price":"14","note":"San Francisco-made; 12 Bay Area botanicals finished in Willett rye casks. Herbal bitterness softened by vanilla, oak, and toasted rye spice.","group":"Fernet"},{"id":"ad:geijer-california-fernet","name":"Geijer California Fernet","price":"12","note":"San Francisco-made (Geijer Spirits); 21 botanicals, corn-base. Minty, bitter, grassier and drier than traditional fernet.","group":"Fernet"},{"id":"ad:amaro--vino--pasubio","name":"Amaro \"Vino\" Pasubio","price":"8","note":"Wine-based alpine amaro from Trentino by Cappelletti; mugo pine, blueberry, and smoky alpine herbs on a red wine base. Rare style.","group":"Amari"},{"id":"ad:amaro-ciociaro","name":"Amaro Ciociaro","price":"8","note":"Lazio, Italy (Paolucci, since 1873); bitter orange backbone with chamomile, anise, and cinnamon. Bittersweet, approachable, lingers with chocolate and spice.","group":"Amari"},{"id":"ad:amaro-sfumato","name":"Amaro Sfumato","price":"10","note":"Trentino, Italy (Cappelletti); smoked Chinese rhubarb — the smoke is real. Deep, dark, earthy, bittersweet with an alpine herbal finish.","group":"Amari"},{"id":"ad:amaro-montenegro","name":"Amaro Montenegro","price":"10","note":"Bologna, Italy; 40 botanicals. Medium bitter, notably aromatic — orange peel, vanilla, coriander, rose petal. The gateway amaro for newcomers.","group":"Amari"},{"id":"ad:amaro-meletti","name":"Amaro Meletti","price":"8","note":"Marche, Italy (since 1870); saffron, violet flower, anise, and orange peel. Caramelized orange and cola sweetness, floral, warm spice.","group":"Amari"},{"id":"ad:amaro-del-etna","name":"Amaro Del Etna","price":"9","note":"Sicily, Italy; 26+ herbs from the slopes of Mount Etna. Bitter citrus, licorice, cinnamon, and a distinct volcanic mineral character.","group":"Amari"},{"id":"ad:amaro-amorino","name":"Amaro Amorino","price":"12","note":"Seattle-made (Letterpress Distilling); named for the founder's Italian grandfather. Seville orange peel, baking spice, vanilla, and floral notes. Light-to-medium bitter.","group":"Amari"},{"id":"ad:amaro-amorino-reserva","name":"Amaro Amorino Reserva","price":"14","note":"Seattle-made (Letterpress Distilling); the reserve expression of Amorino. Same orange-spice-floral DNA, deeper and more layered from extended aging.","group":"Amari"},{"id":"ad:amaro-nonino","name":"Amaro Nonino","price":"14","note":"Friuli, Italy (Nonino Distillery); grappa-based amaro aged in small oak barrels. Fruity and elegant — apricot, caramel, orange, gentian. One of the great Italian amari.","group":"Amari"},{"id":"ad:amaro-cansiglio","name":"Amaro Cansiglio","price":"8","note":"Veneto, Italy; 18 herbs and berries from the ancient Cansiglio forest in the Dolomite foothills. Mint, pine, bitter orange, eucalyptus — fresh and invigorating.","group":"Amari"},{"id":"ad:maraska-pelinkovac","name":"Maraska Pelinkovac","price":"10","note":"Croatian wormwood liqueur; Dalmatia. Intensely bitter and herbal, dark fruit and dried spice, medicinal and unapologetic.","group":"Amari"},{"id":"ad:cynar","name":"Cynar","price":"9","note":"Italy (Campari Group, since 1952); artichoke-led, 13 herbs. Vegetal, bittersweet — dark chocolate, walnut, caramel with a cola-like sweetness. Surprisingly approachable.","group":"Amari"},{"id":"ad:braulio","name":"Braulio","price":"10","note":"Valtellina, Italy (since 1875 in Bormio, 4,000 ft altitude); juniper, wormwood, gentian, yarrow. Cooling piney character, mountain violet, mint, bittersweet alpine finish.","group":"Amari"},{"id":"ad:amaro-brucato--orchard","name":"Amaro Brucato \"Orchard\"","price":"12","note":"San Francisco-made (Brucato Spirits); apricot, walnut, Meyer lemon, and cinnamon. Juicy, fruit-forward, warm baking spice. Think of it as a fruity California Campari.","group":"Amari"},{"id":"ad:amaro-brucato--chaparral","name":"Amaro Brucato \"Chaparral\"","price":"12","note":"San Francisco-made (Brucato Spirits); yerba santa, spearmint, cardamom. Herbal, minty, Chartreuse-like character. Coastal California hillsides in a glass.","group":"Amari"},{"id":"ad:amaro-brucato--fields","name":"Amaro Brucato \"Fields\"","price":"12","note":"San Francisco-made (Brucato Spirits); artichoke leaf, beet root, rhubarb root, fenugreek, dill. Savory and herbal over caramel, soft bitterness.","group":"Amari"},{"id":"ad:nonino-grappa-di-merlot","name":"Nonino Grappa di Merlot","price":"18","note":"Friuli, Italy (Nonino Distillery); single-varietal pomace grappa from Merlot. Rose petal, ripe cherry, soft and velvety texture, delicate almond finish.","group":"Grappa & brandy"},{"id":"ad:nonino-grappa-di-moscato","name":"Nonino Grappa di Moscato","price":"18","note":"Friuli, Italy (Nonino Distillery); single-varietal pomace grappa from Moscato. Highly aromatic — rose, sage, vanilla, orange blossom. The most perfumed of the grappas.","group":"Grappa & brandy"},{"id":"ad:martel--blue-swift--cognac","name":"Martel \"Blue Swift\" Cognac","price":"12","note":"Martell VSOP eaux-de-vie finished in Kentucky bourbon casks. Cognac fruit (fig, plum, pear) layered with vanilla, coconut, and toasted oak from the bourbon barrels.","group":"Grappa & brandy"},{"id":"ad:menorvall-calvados","name":"Menorvall Calvados","price":"12","note":"Normandy, France; apple brandy, 40% ABV. Ripe pear aroma, baking spice, chamomile, sandalwood. Apple brandy that drinks closer to Armagnac than cider.","group":"Grappa & brandy"},{"id":"ad:compass-box-artist-s-blend-scotch","name":"Compass Box Artist's Blend Scotch","price":"14","note":"Blended Scotch by Compass Box (UK); light and approachable. Butterscotch, toffee apple, vanilla, light fruit, creamy mouthfeel. Un-chill filtered, 43% ABV.","group":"Whiskey"},{"id":"ad:glenlivet-12yr","name":"Glenlivet 12yr","price":"15","note":"Speyside single malt Scotch, Scotland. Fruit-forward and gentle — apple, pear, vanilla, light honey. No peat. The definitive entry-level Speyside.","group":"Whiskey"},{"id":"ad:laphroaig-10yr","name":"Laphroaig 10yr","price":"14","note":"Islay single malt Scotch, Scotland. The most polarizing whisky on the list. Intense peat smoke, seaweed, iodine, and medicinal herbs over a surprising sweetness. Malted barley dried over Islay peat fires.","group":"Whiskey"},{"id":"ad:lagavulin-16yr","name":"Lagavulin 16yr","price":"25","note":"Islay single malt Scotch, Scotland. Peat smoke with far more depth than Laphroaig — sherry richness, dark fruit, black tea, maritime brine, and a long smoky finish. The benchmark peated Scotch.","group":"Whiskey"},{"id":"ad:weller-12yr","name":"Weller 12yr","price":"25","note":"Kentucky straight bourbon, Buffalo Trace; wheated mash bill (wheat replaces rye). Exceptionally smooth — butterscotch, baked apple, cinnamon, vanilla, long oaky finish. Same wheat recipe as Pappy Van Winkle.","group":"Whiskey"}],"flights":[{"name":"It's Not What You'd Expect!","price":"18","desc":"Three amari that wrong-foot you. A California amaro built on artichoke and beet, a Puglian elixir from 1840 that tastes like coffee and chocolate, and a fernet from Brooklyn, not Milan. For the guest who's sure they know amaro.","pours":[{"name":"Amaro Brucato \"Fields\"","origin":"S.F., CA","note":"Brucato's fourth amaro, built from farm-field ingredients: artichoke leaf, beet root, rhubarb root and toasted dandelion, with fenugreek, dill and coriander. Macerated in California grape brandy and rested on toasted oak. Savory and herbal over caramel, softly bitter."},{"name":"Borsci San Marzano","origin":"Puglia, IT","note":"Elisir Borsci San Marzano, a recipe dating to 1840 and Giuseppe Borsci, now made by Distilleria Caffo. Melissa, wormwood, clove and saffron. Sweet and dark up front, then coffee, chocolate and orange, with a velvety bitter finish. Traditionally poured over ice cream or into coffee."},{"name":"Faccia Brutto Fernet","origin":"Brooklyn, NY","note":"Brooklyn-made fernet in the old Italian style — bracing, minty, properly bitter. The producer's name means 'ugly face,' which is roughly what the first sip makes."}]},{"name":"Out of the Woods","price":"18","desc":"A walk through three forests: the Italian Alps, a Seattle barrel room, and California woodland. Piney, dark, resinous — the deep end of the genre.","pours":[{"name":"Amaro Braulio","origin":"Valtellina, IT","note":"The alpine classic, made in Bormio high in the Italian Alps since 1875 — juniper, gentian, wormwood, and yarrow, rested in oak. Tastes like pine air after snow."},{"name":"Amaro Amorino Reserva","origin":"Seattle, WA","note":"Letterpress Distilling, Seattle. Seville orange peel, baking spice, and florals, with the reserve bottling rested in used whiskey barrels at a fuller proof. Named for the founder's Abruzzo-born grandfather."},{"name":"Brucato Amaro Woodlands","origin":"S.F., CA","note":"San Francisco amaro from California botanicals — elderberry, cocoa nib, black fig, and citrus. Dark, woodsy, gently sweet."}]},{"name":"The Hill I Die On","price":"18","desc":"Hillside botany with strong opinions — California chaparral scrub, a Piedmontese pharmacist's 25-botanical formula, and a fernet that picks a fight. Order it if you have convictions.","pours":[{"name":"Brucato Amaro \"Chaparral\"","origin":"S.F., CA","note":"Brucato's ode to California's chaparral scrubland — yerba santa, bay laurel, spearmint, cardamom. Green and herbal, pointed in the direction of Chartreuse."},{"name":"Amaro Foro","origin":"Piedmont, IT","note":"From Piedmont, formulated by a former pharmacist — 25 botanicals over a citrus-peel core, angelica to chamomile. Dark, nutty, bittersweet."},{"name":"California Fernet","origin":"Petaluma, CA","note":"A California take on the most divisive amaro — long botanical maceration, mint-forward and brighter than its Milanese ancestors, bottled at a full 40%."}]}],"cocktails":[{"name":"Minivan Mega-Fun","price":"15","build":"tequila blanco, blistered shishito pepper simple, veso tomato vermouth, lime, gran classico, soda","ingredients":[{"name":"Tequila blanco","description":"Unaged agave spirit from Mexico: bright, peppery and vegetal. The base of the drink."},{"name":"Blistered shishito pepper simple","description":"The composition of this blistered shishito pepper simple has not been specified for this prep. Confirm with your bar lead before clearing it for any allergy."},{"name":"Veso tomato vermouth","description":"A tomato vermouth from Veso."},{"name":"Lime","description":"Fresh lime."},{"name":"Gran Classico","description":"A Swiss-made bitter aperitivo in the Campari family: bittersweet, with orange peel and herbs."},{"name":"Soda","description":"Soda water top for lift and length."}]},{"name":"Oh My Word!","price":"16","build":"gin, aperol, peach and nectarine maceration, lemon, grapefruit bitters","ingredients":[{"name":"Gin","description":"Juniper-forward base spirit."},{"name":"Aperol","description":"Italian bitter-orange aperitivo: lighter, sweeter and lower in alcohol than Campari, with orange and rhubarb."},{"name":"Peach and nectarine maceration","description":"The composition of this peach and nectarine maceration has not been specified for this prep. Confirm with your bar lead before clearing it for any allergy."},{"name":"Lemon","description":"Fresh lemon."},{"name":"Grapefruit bitters","description":"Aromatic bitters flavored with grapefruit peel."}]},{"name":"San Fermo Negroni","price":"17","build":"gin, campari, amaricano bianco, yzaguirre barrel aged sweet vermouth, rose tincture, grapefruit expression","ingredients":[{"name":"Gin","amount":"1 oz","description":"Juniper-forward base spirit; gives the Negroni its botanical backbone."},{"name":"Campari","amount":"1 oz","description":"The iconic Italian bitter aperitivo — bright red, orange-and-rhubarb bitter, the soul of a Negroni.","highlight":"the bitter"},{"name":"Amaricano Bianco","amount":"0.5 oz","description":"A clear American-made bitter aperitivo (Fast Penny, Seattle); softer and more floral than Campari, it lightens the build.","highlight":"Seattle"},{"name":"Yzaguirre barrel-aged sweet vermouth","amount":"1 oz","description":"A Spanish (Catalan) sweet vermouth rested in barrel — richer, rounder, and spicier than standard rosso, adding depth and a long finish."},{"name":"Rose tincture","amount":"barspoon","description":"A house infusion of rose; a whisper of floral aromatics lifts the nose."},{"name":"Grapefruit expression","amount":"garnish","description":"Oils squeezed from a grapefruit peel over the surface — citrus aromatics to greet the first sip."}]},{"name":"Imaginary Friend","price":"18","build":"mezcal, spiced red wine and balsamic agrodolce, amaro lucano, lime, veso fig leaf vermouth, black lemon bitters","ingredients":[{"name":"Mezcal","description":"A Mexican spirit distilled from roasted agave. Unlike tequila, the agave piñas are pit-roasted over hot rocks before fermentation, giving mezcal its signature smokiness. Earthy, complex, and layered — vegetal sweetness under the smoke, with a long finish. The base of the whole cocktail."},{"name":"Spiced red wine and balsamic agrodolce","description":"The composition of this spiced red wine and balsamic agrodolce has not been specified for this prep. Confirm with your bar lead before clearing it for any allergy."},{"name":"Amaro Lucano","description":"A bittersweet amaro from Basilicata in southern Italy, made since 1894 from a blend of herbs."},{"name":"Lime","description":"Fresh lime."},{"name":"Veso fig leaf vermouth","description":"A fig-leaf vermouth from Veso."},{"name":"Black lemon bitters","description":"Bitters built on black lemon, the dried, darkened citrus of Middle Eastern cooking: smoky, sour and earthy."}]},{"name":"Grandma's Ash Tray","price":"19","build":"bourbon, martell cognac, yzaguirre rose vermouth, amaro rucolino, mr. black, lavender bitters","ingredients":[{"name":"Bourbon","description":"American whiskey, corn-led: the base of the drink."},{"name":"Martell cognac","description":"French grape brandy from the house of Martell, founded in 1715."},{"name":"Yzaguirre rose vermouth","description":"A Spanish rosado vermouth from Yzaguirre in Catalonia."},{"name":"Amaro Rucolino","description":"An Italian amaro made from arugula (rucola): peppery, green and bittersweet."},{"name":"Mr. Black","description":"An Australian cold-brew coffee liqueur, drier and more coffee-forward than most."},{"name":"Lavender bitters","description":"Aromatic bitters flavored with lavender."}]},{"name":"Ya sure you betcha","price":"16","build":"white port, mancino cherry blossom vermouth, veso alpine vermouth, blueberry and lavender maceration, lemon, soda, prosecco","ingredients":[{"name":"White port","description":"Portuguese fortified wine made from white grapes."},{"name":"Mancino cherry blossom vermouth","description":"A cherry blossom vermouth from Mancino, an Italian vermouth house."},{"name":"Veso alpine vermouth","description":"An alpine vermouth from Veso."},{"name":"Blueberry and lavender maceration","description":"The composition of this blueberry and lavender maceration has not been specified for this prep. Confirm with your bar lead before clearing it for any allergy."},{"name":"Lemon","description":"Fresh lemon."},{"name":"Soda","description":"Soda water top for lift and length."},{"name":"Prosecco","description":"Italian sparkling wine, topped over the drink for fizz."}]}],"dishes":[{"id":"dish:sea-wolf-bakery-baguette","name":"Sea Wolf Bakery Baguette","section":"Antipasti","desc":"les prés salés butter, sea salt","price":"8","ingredients":[{"term":"Sea Wolf Bakers","pronunciation":null,"note":"Seattle's Fremont neighborhood bakery, founded 2014 by brothers Jesse and Kit Schumann. All breads are naturally leavened using Pacific Northwest grains. Named one of the 100 best bakeries in the U.S. by Food & Wine."},{"term":"Les prés salés butter","pronunciation":"lay pray sah-LAY","note":"Belgian-churned cultured butter, 82% butterfat, finished with hand-harvested coarse sea salt crystals from the Camargue — a salt marsh region in the south of France. Richer and more complex than standard salted butter."},{"term":"Maldon sea salt","pronunciation":null,"note":"Flaky finishing salt from Essex, England. Prized for its pyramid-shaped crystals, clean flavor, and satisfying crunch."}],"allergens":["gluten","dairy"],"pron":null,"story":"A baguette from Sea Wolf Bakers in Seattle's Fremont neighborhood, one of the Pacific Northwest's most celebrated naturally leavened bakeries. Baked the same day, served with Belgian-churned les prés salés butter flecked with coarse Camargue sea salt crystals. Simple and non-negotiable — this one never changes.","can_modify":[],"allergen_note":null,"raw":false},{"id":"dish:castelvetrano-olives","name":"Castelvetrano Olives","section":"Antipasti","desc":"fennel pollen, citrus, rosemary","price":"6","ingredients":[{"term":"Castelvetrano olives","pronunciation":"kah-stel-veh-TRAH-noh","note":"Nocellara del Belice variety, named for the Sicilian town of Castelvetrano. PDO-protected, so they can only come from the Valle del Belice. Lye-cured for a clean, buttery flavor with no bitterness — the least aggressive olive on any table."},{"term":"Fennel pollen","pronunciation":null,"note":"The fine yellow pollen harvested from wild fennel flowers. Intensely aromatic, floral, and anise-forward — used here as a finishing garnish."}],"allergens":["allium"],"pron":"kah-stel-veh-TRAH-noh","story":"Meaty Nocellara del Belice olives from the Valle del Belice in southwestern Sicily — one of the only olive varieties with a Protected Designation of Origin. Marinated in-house with citrus peel, garlic, chili flake, and olive oil, then finished with a dusting of fennel pollen and rosemary. Bright, buttery, mild.","can_modify":[],"allergen_note":"Confirmed: the marinade carries garlic (allium) and chili flake. The garlic is marinated in, so it can't be picked out — off limits for an allium allergy. The chili flake also adds a little heat, a heads up for spice-sensitive guests.","raw":false},{"id":"dish:marcona-almonds","name":"Marcona Almonds","section":"Antipasti","desc":"rosemary olive oil, fried rosemary, maldon sea salt","price":"6","ingredients":[{"term":"Marcona almonds","pronunciation":"mar-KOH-nah","note":"A Spanish almond variety grown along the Mediterranean coast — Alicante, Valencia, and Tarragona. Rounder and plumper than California almonds, with a higher oil content and a naturally sweet, buttery flavor. Spain is the primary producer."},{"term":"Maldon sea salt","pronunciation":null,"note":"Flaky finishing salt from Essex, England — same as on the bread. Tossed on with the rosemary oil after roasting so the crunch stays distinct."}],"allergens":["nuts"],"pron":"mar-KOH-nah","story":"Imported Marcona almonds from Spain's Mediterranean coast, roasted until golden, tossed in rosemary olive oil and Maldon sea salt, then finished with crisp fried rosemary. Rounder and softer than a California almond, with a buttery sweetness that makes them genuinely hard to stop eating.","can_modify":[],"allergen_note":null,"raw":false},{"id":"dish:farinata","name":"Farinata","section":"Antipasti","desc":"chickpea flatbread, house cagliata, seasonal preserve, fresh thyme, sea salt","price":"13","ingredients":[{"term":"Farinata","pronunciation":"fah-ree-NAH-tah","note":"Ancient Ligurian flatbread made from chickpea flour, water, olive oil, and salt — baked in a very hot cast iron pan until the edges are crisp and the center is custardy. Also called cecìna in Tuscany. Naturally gluten-free."},{"term":"Cagliatta","pronunciation":"kal-YAH-tah","note":"Our housemade fresh cheese — whole milk curdled with lemon juice, then dressed with rosemary-infused olive oil and salt. A fresh, mild, milky curd; closer to a soft ricotta than a mozzarella. Made in-house."},{"term":"Rhubarb caramelized onion jam","pronunciation":null,"note":"Seasonal preserve made in-house: rhubarb cooked down with caramelized onions, vinegar, and sugar. Tart, sweet, savory — made to work against the richness of the cagliatta."}],"allergens":["dairy"],"pron":"fah-ree-NAH-tah","story":"A Ligurian chickpea flatbread with roots in 13th-century Genoa — thin, crisp at the edges, soft and nutty in the center. Made in-house from chickpea flour, rosemary olive oil, and water, finished with fresh thyme and sea salt. The housemade cagliatta and a seasonal preserve sit alongside on the plate. Current preserve: rhubarb caramelized onion jam.","can_modify":["dairy"],"allergen_note":"Naturally gluten-free — chickpea flour, no wheat. The cagliatta is plated alongside; for dairy-free it moves to a bowl on the side or comes off entirely, and the preserve stays.","raw":false},{"id":"dish:house-caesar","name":"House Caesar","section":"Antipasti","desc":"anchovy, parmigiano reggiano, red wine croutons","price":"14","ingredients":[{"term":"Boquerones","pronunciation":"boh-keh-ROH-nez","note":"White anchovies cured in vinegar rather than salt — mild, tender, and bright. Distinct from the oil-packed brown anchovies in the dressing; these sit on top as a finishing garnish."},{"term":"Parmigiano-Reggiano","pronunciation":"par-mee-JAH-noh reh-JAH-noh","note":"Aged Italian hard cheese from the Parma region, with PDO protection. Used both in the dressing (grated) and shaved over the top. Nutty, salty, and complex."},{"term":"Red wine croutons","pronunciation":null,"note":"Made in-house: bread soaked in a red wine, butter, garlic, and rosemary mixture, then dehydrated until crisp all the way through. Deeply savory, and the crunch holds up in the dressing."},{"term":"Coddled egg dressing","pronunciation":null,"note":"The dressing is built with egg yolk — emulsified raw with oil and champagne vinegar for a thick, rich, classic Caesar consistency. Not cooked through, similar to mayonnaise. It also carries the anchovy: for fish allergies the dressing has to go, not just the boquerones on top."}],"allergens":["fish","dairy","gluten","egg","allium"],"pron":null,"story":"Romaine dressed with a classic from-scratch Caesar dressing — champagne vinegar, anchovy, garlic, Parmigiano-Reggiano, egg yolk, Worcestershire, Dijon, black pepper. Topped with boquerones white anchovies and red wine croutons: bread soaked in red wine, butter, garlic, and rosemary, then dehydrated until crisp the whole way through. A clean, honest Caesar.","can_modify":["fish","dairy","gluten","egg","allium"],"allergen_note":"Every flag has a path, but fish takes two cuts: the dressing itself has anchovy in it, so pulling the boquerones alone doesn't clear it — omit the boquerones AND swap the dressing for oil and vinegar. Croutons on the side handles gluten. The same oil-and-vinegar swap with cheese on the side covers dairy, egg, and allium. Fully modified it becomes a clean romaine salad with oil and vinegar; tell the guest what they're getting.","raw":true},{"id":"dish:farm-house-salad","name":"Farm House Salad","section":"Antipasti","desc":"mixed greens, apple, shaved fennel, bread and butter pickled onion, candied almonds, apple vinaigrette","price":"18","ingredients":[{"term":"Bread and butter pickled onion","pronunciation":null,"note":"A sweet-and-tangy pickle style — onions cured in a vinegar brine with sugar and spice. Despite the name there's no bread or butter; it refers to the classic sweet pickle profile. Bright and sharp against the greens."},{"term":"Shaved fennel","pronunciation":null,"note":"The crisp anise-scented bulb, shaved thin and raw. Adds a clean, fresh crunch. No allergens of its own."},{"term":"Candied almonds","pronunciation":null,"note":"Our candied almonds are slivered almonds tossed with powdered sugar and salt — nothing else, so no dairy or egg. Sweet and crisp, a textural contrast in the salad. Guests with tree nut allergies need to know these are here."},{"term":"Apple vinaigrette","pronunciation":null,"note":"Apple cider vinegar and lemon juice blended with green peppercorn, mustard, and shallot, emulsified with canola oil and stabilized with xanthan gum. Carries allium from the shallot, and mustard — which we do not track as a chip, so mention it for a mustard allergy."}],"allergens":["nuts","allium"],"pron":null,"story":"Mixed baby lettuces with shaved fennel and fresh apple, brightened by crisp bread and butter pickled onions. Candied slivered almonds add sweetness and crunch, and an apple vinaigrette ties the fruit through the dressing.","can_modify":["nuts"],"allergen_note":"Tree nuts: the candied almonds come off cleanly for a nut allergy. Allium: the pickled onion and the shallot in the vinaigrette run through the salad, not removable. The vinaigrette also carries mustard, which we do not track as a chip — flag it for a mustard allergy. The candied almonds are just slivered almonds, powdered sugar, and salt — no dairy or egg.","raw":false},{"id":"dish:burrata","name":"Burrata","section":"Antipasti","desc":"Swan House Farm heirloom tomatoes, basil, tomato leaf oil, aged balsamic, crostini","price":"20","ingredients":[{"term":"Burrata","pronunciation":"boo-RAH-tah","note":"a fresh cow's-milk cheese from Puglia: a mozzarella shell holding a center of stracciatella (cheese curds) and cream. Cut into it and the middle should flow."},{"term":"Tomato leaf oil","pronunciation":null,"note":"Made in-house by blending tomato leaves into neutral canola oil and straining it clear, capturing the grassy, green flavor of the plant itself rather than the fruit. Just leaves and oil — no allergens of its own."},{"term":"Aged balsamic","pronunciation":null,"note":"Balsamic vinegar aged until thick and sweet — cooked grape must and time, nothing else. No allergens of its own."}],"allergens":["dairy","gluten"],"pron":"boo-RAH-tah","story":"Creamy burrata with heirloom tomatoes from Swan House Farm and fresh basil, drizzled with tomato leaf oil and aged balsamic. Crostini on the side for texture.","can_modify":["gluten"],"allergen_note":"Dairy: the burrata is the dish, not removable. Gluten: crostini come on the side and come off cleanly. The tomato leaf oil is just tomato leaves and canola oil — no allium anywhere on the plate.","raw":false},{"id":"dish:grilled-corn-gazpacho","name":"Grilled Corn Gazpacho","section":"Antipasti","desc":"grilled corn, tomato, bell pepper, cucumber, onion, champagne vinegar, crème fraîche, red onion agro dolce, micro basil","price":"15","ingredients":[{"term":"Gazpacho","pronunciation":"gahz-PAH-choh","note":"A cold, blended Spanish soup of vegetables and acid, served chilled. This version leans on grilled corn for sweetness and smoke against the tomato and vinegar."},{"term":"Corn cob stock","pronunciation":null,"note":"A light stock simmered from the stripped corn cobs, deepening the corn flavor without dairy or meat. No allergens of its own."},{"term":"Crème fraîche","pronunciation":"krem FRESH","note":"French cultured cream — thicker and tangier than sour cream, milder than Greek yogurt. Spooned on top for richness and a cool counterpoint to the acid."},{"term":"Agro dolce","pronunciation":"AH-groh DOHL-cheh","note":"Italian for sour-sweet: red onion cooked down in butter with vinegar and sugar into a sharp, bright pickle-like garnish. Carries allium and dairy from the butter."}],"allergens":["dairy","allium"],"pron":"gahz-PAH-choh","story":"Grilled corn sweetness anchors this cold soup. Tomato, bell pepper, cucumber, and onion are puréed with champagne vinegar and corn cob stock, finished with olive oil and white pepper. Crème fraîche cools the top, red onion agro dolce adds sharp pickle and bite, and micro basil finishes it.","can_modify":["dairy"],"allergen_note":"Naturally gluten-free. Dairy: two places — the crème fraîche and the butter in the red onion agro dolce. Both are garnishes and come off cleanly together for a dairy allergy. Allium: onion is puréed into the base and the agro dolce sits on top — the garnish can come off, the base cannot.","raw":false},{"id":"dish:swan-house-bolognese","name":"Swan House Bolognese","section":"Primi & Secondi","desc":"saffron spaghetti, swan house beef & pork, tomato, fennel, rosemary, parmigiano reggiano","price":"32","ingredients":[{"term":"Bolognese","pronunciation":"boh-loh-NYEH-zeh","note":"a slow-cooked meat ragù from Bologna in Emilia-Romagna — traditionally more meat than tomato."},{"term":"Saffron","pronunciation":"SAF-ron","note":"the dried stigma of the crocus flower, hand-harvested and prized. We brew it into a concentrated tea, strain it, and use it in place of water in the extruded dough — gold color, hay-like earthiness, and the reason this golden pasta has no egg."},{"term":"Parmigiano Reggiano","pronunciation":"par-mee-JAH-noh reh-JAH-noh","note":"the aged hard cheese from Emilia-Romagna, the only 'real' Parmesan; nutty, savory, crystalline."}],"allergens":["gluten","dairy","allium"],"pron":"boh-loh-NYEH-zeh","story":"The family recipe — on the menu since day one, and it never leaves. This run is built on ground beef and pork raised at Swan House Farm; when it's gone, the dish returns to the classic pork-and-veal build. Either way: seasoned with our own spice blend, cooked down slowly with red wine, tomato, fennel, and rosemary, then finished with cream, butter, sherry, and salt. The saffron spaghetti is extruded with a strained, highly concentrated saffron tea in place of water — that's where the gold color and the subtle earthy aroma come from, no egg involved.","can_modify":["dairy"],"allergen_note":"No egg in the saffron spaghetti. Dairy: the kitchen can finish it without cream, butter, or Parmigiano — a leaner, brighter ragù. Built in and non-negotiable: allium, the cooked red wine, chili flake, and black pepper.","raw":false},{"id":"dish:eggplant-parm","name":"Eggplant Parm","section":"Primi & Secondi","desc":"breaded fried eggplant, mozzarella, rigatoni, eggplant pomodoro, parmesan, basil","price":"31","ingredients":[{"term":"Rigatoni","pronunciation":"ree-gah-TOH-nee","note":"Ridged tube pasta — the ridges and hollow centers grab and hold the sauce. Standard durum-wheat pasta, so it carries gluten."},{"term":"Eggplant pomodoro sauce","pronunciation":"poh-moh-DOH-roh","note":"Our house pomodoro base — garlic, onion, and tomato — with eggplant cooked down into it. No cream, no meat. Carries allium from the garlic and onion."},{"term":"Breading","pronunciation":null,"note":"Flour, egg wash, and breadcrumb on the fried eggplant. Carries gluten and egg, both built in."},{"term":"Mozzarella","pronunciation":"moht-sah-REH-lah","note":"Melted over the fried eggplant in the oven. Carries dairy, as does the Parmesan finish."}],"allergens":["gluten","dairy","egg","allium"],"pron":null,"story":"Eggplant cut thick and salted overnight to draw out bitterness, then breaded and fried. It goes into the oven under mozzarella to melt, and lands on a bed of rigatoni tossed in an eggplant pomodoro sauce. Finished with Parmesan and basil.","can_modify":[],"allergen_note":"Gluten: the breading and the rigatoni, both built in. Dairy: mozzarella and Parmesan, built in. Egg: the egg wash in the breading, built in. Allium: garlic and onion in the pomodoro base, not removable.","raw":false},{"id":"dish:stuffed-farfalle","name":"Stuffed Farfalle","section":"Primi & Secondi","desc":"egg pasta stuffed with basil, ricotta & mozzarella, spicy vodka sauce, heirloom cherry tomatoes, basil","price":"30","ingredients":[{"term":"Farfalle","note":"Bow-tie shaped pasta; here made in-house from egg dough and stuffed with basil, ricotta, and mozzarella. A fresh egg dough, so it carries egg and wheat.","pronunciation":"far-FAH-leh"},{"term":"Chiffonade","pronunciation":"shif-oh-NAHD","note":"Herbs sliced into thin ribbons — here fresh basil, scattered over the top."},{"note":"A tomato-and-cream sauce with some heat: vodka, crushed tomato, heavy cream, onion, garlic, chili flake, parmesan, butter and salt.","pronunciation":null,"term":"Spicy vodka sauce"}],"allergens":["gluten","egg","dairy","allium"],"pron":"far-FAH-leh","story":"Bow-tie pasta made from egg dough, filled with basil, ricotta, and fresh mozzarella. Finished in a spicy vodka sauce built on onion and garlic, topped with halved heirloom cherry tomatoes and fresh basil chiffonade. Warm, creamy, and a little sharp from the heat.","can_modify":[],"allergen_note":"Egg and gluten are both in the fresh egg pasta — built in. Dairy runs through the filling (ricotta, mozzarella) and the cream in the sauce, so it can't come off this dish. Allium: onion and garlic are cooked into the vodka sauce.","raw":false},{"id":"dish:mafaldine-alla-carbonara","name":"Mafaldine alla Carbonara","section":"Primi & Secondi","desc":"guanciale, black pepper, swan house farm egg yolk, pecorino romano","price":"28","ingredients":[{"term":"Mafaldine","pronunciation":"mah-fal-DEE-neh","note":"Wide, flat pasta with ruffled edges on both sides, from Naples. Named in honor of Princess Mafalda of Savoy — also called reginette (little queens). The wavy edges grip the carbonara sauce exceptionally well."},{"term":"Carbonara","pronunciation":"kar-boh-NAH-rah","note":"a Roman sauce of egg, hard cheese, cured pork, and black pepper — emulsified, never creamed."},{"term":"Guanciale","pronunciation":"gwahn-CHAH-leh","note":"cured pork jowl, fattier and more delicate than pancetta; renders down into the sauce."},{"term":"Pecorino Romano","pronunciation":"peh-koh-REE-noh roh-MAH-noh","note":"a hard, salty sheep's-milk cheese from Lazio — sharper than Parmigiano."}],"allergens":["gluten","egg","dairy"],"pron":"mah-fal-DEE-neh","story":"The Roman classic on mafaldine — wide, ruffled ribbon pasta named after Princess Mafalda of Savoy — whose wavy edges catch and hold the glossy sauce. Rendered guanciale, lots of cracked black pepper, Pecorino Romano, and a Swan House Farm egg yolk emulsified into a peppery, silky coating. No cream — the silk comes from the egg and cheese. Served with a raw yolk, hence the asterisk.","can_modify":["dairy","egg"],"allergen_note":"All the mods exist, none are recommended. Guanciale can come off for vegetarian — the kitchen subs in whatever vegetable is running seasonally on the menu. Dairy or even the egg can technically be left out, at which point it's pasta and pepper; we get the no-egg request more than we'd like, so set expectations honestly. No allium in the dish.","raw":true},{"id":"dish:risotto-alle-zucchine","name":"Risotto alle Zucchine","section":"Primi & Secondi","desc":"roasted zucchini, parmesan, crème fraîche-basil puree, lemon","price":"28","ingredients":[{"term":"Risotto alle Zucchine","pronunciation":"ree-ZOH-toh AHL-leh zoo-kee-NEH","note":"Italian for 'risotto with zucchini.' A summer take on the dish — roasted zucchini folded through the rice, finished bright with lemon and basil."},{"term":"Basil puree","pronunciation":null,"note":"Basil, zucchini, crème fraîche, and lemon oil blended smooth into a vivid green puree. Herbaceous and bright with a little richness from the crème fraîche — swirled in at the finish, with micro basil on top for aroma. Note the crème fraîche: this puree carries dairy."},{"term":"Arborio","pronunciation":"ar-BOR-ee-oh","note":"Short-grain Italian rice that releases starch as it cooks, creating risotto's signature creamy texture. Par-cooked in batches at the restaurant, finished per plate to order."}],"allergens":["dairy","allium"],"pron":"ree-ZOH-toh AHL-leh zoo-kee-NEH","story":"Creamy risotto built on roasted zucchini, garlic, and shallot. Finished with butter, Parmesan, and a basil puree blended from basil, zucchini, crème fraîche, and lemon oil. Bright, clean, and seasonal.","can_modify":["dairy"],"allergen_note":"Naturally gluten-free. Dairy lives in three spots: Parmesan, butter, and the crème fraîche in the basil puree — a true dairy-free plate means dropping all three, including the puree, so flag it to the kitchen. Allium and the cooked wine can't come out — our risotto base is always par-cooked with onion and white wine in the stock, and this build adds garlic and shallot.","raw":false},{"id":"dish:grilled-king-salmon","name":"Grilled King Salmon","section":"Primi & Secondi","desc":"Troll-caught Washington king salmon, Bibb lettuce, roasted tomato vinaigrette, sun gold tomatoes, charred zucchini","price":"38","ingredients":[{"note":"Caught one fish at a time on hook and line rather than by net, which keeps the fillet intact and the flesh firm. King (chinook) is the richest of the Pacific salmon, high in fat with a deep colour. Seven ounce fillet, grilled to medium rare.","pronunciation":null,"term":"Troll-caught king salmon"},{"note":"A soft butterhead lettuce with loose, tender leaves and a mild sweetness. Dressed and served cool against the warm fish.","pronunciation":null,"term":"Bibb lettuce"},{"pronunciation":null,"term":"Sun gold tomatoes","note":"A small orange cherry tomato, noticeably sweeter and lower in acid than a red cherry tomato. Served raw."},{"term":"Roasted tomato vinaigrette","pronunciation":null,"note":"Our roasted tomato vinaigrette starts with tomatoes, quartered and roasted low and slow at 200° until they lose about 60% of their moisture, which concentrates their flavor and natural sweetness. We finish the roasted tomatoes with red wine vinegar, shallot, and extra-virgin olive oil to make the vinaigrette, seasoned with oregano and black pepper. Guests should know this is a cooked vinaigrette, not raw, so it has deep roasted tomato flavor rather than bright acidity."},{"term":"Marigold petals","note":"Edible flower petals, faintly citrus and peppery. A garnish, and they come off on request.","pronunciation":null}],"allergens":["fish","allium"],"pron":null,"story":"Seven ounces of troll-caught Washington king salmon, grilled to medium rare. Served with Bibb lettuce dressed in roasted tomato vinaigrette, bright sun gold tomatoes, and charred zucchini. Finished with marigold petals.","can_modify":[],"allergen_note":"Fish: king salmon, central to the dish, cannot come off. Allium: shallot is in the roasted tomato vinaigrette. Served medium rare.","raw":true},{"id":"dish:roasted-duck-breast","name":"Roasted Duck Breast","section":"Primi & Secondi","desc":"Muscovy duck breast, polenta, chanterelles, corn, huckleberry demi-glace","price":"42","ingredients":[{"term":"Muscovy duck","note":"A leaner breed than Pekin, with a deeper, less fatty flavour closer to red meat than poultry. The breast is rendered skin-side down so the fat runs off and the skin crisps, then cooked to medium rare.","pronunciation":"MUSS-koh-vee"},{"term":"Chanterelles","pronunciation":"shan-tuh-RELS","note":"A wild golden mushroom, firm with a mild apricot-and-pepper note. Sautéed here."},{"term":"Polenta","note":"Ground cornmeal cooked until thick and creamy, the bed for the duck. The house polenta is built with cream, stock and butter, so treat it as dairy unless the kitchen says this prep differs.","pronunciation":"poh-LEN-tah"},{"note":"A soft herb in the oregano family, sweeter and more floral than oregano. Used fresh to lift the plate.","pronunciation":"MAR-jer-um","term":"Marjoram"},{"term":"Huckleberry demi-glace","pronunciation":"DEH-mee glahss","note":"The huckleberry demi-glace is chicken stock reduced until glossy and thick, then finished with cooked huckleberries. Guests get a rich, slightly sweet sauce with the tartness of the berries. It's not overly sweet, just balanced fruit with deep savory stock."}],"allergens":["dairy","allium"],"pron":null,"story":"Muscovy duck breast rendered skin-side down to medium rare. It sits on creamy polenta with sautéed chanterelles, corn, and shallot. Marjoram and sherry vinegar brighten the plate. A huckleberry demi-glace finishes it, adding dark fruit and depth.","can_modify":["dairy"],"allergen_note":"Dairy: the polenta is built with cream and butter and can be swapped for a dairy-free starch. Allium: shallot is cooked into the mushroom and corn garnish. Served medium rare.","raw":true},{"id":"dish:earl-grey-tiramisu","name":"Earl Grey Tiramisu","section":"Dessert","desc":"mascarpone, ladyfingers, miro tea, planteray dark rum, bergamot agrumato","price":"15","ingredients":[{"term":"Tiramisu","pronunciation":"tee-rah-mee-SOO","note":"Classic Northern Italian dessert — ladyfinger biscuits soaked in liquid, layered with sweetened mascarpone cream, finished with cocoa. The name means \"pick me up\" in Italian."},{"term":"Miro Tea","pronunciation":null,"note":"Ballard neighborhood tea shop, Seattle, founded 2005. Carries over 200 varieties of loose leaf tea. We use their Earl Grey for the ladyfinger soak — clean, bergamot-forward, and bright."},{"term":"Bergamot agrumato","pronunciation":"BEHR-gah-mot ag-roo-MAH-toh","note":"Extra virgin olive oil co-milled with whole bergamot fruit at the press — the citrus oils are captured inside the olive oil rather than infused afterward. Bergamot is the citrus that gives Earl Grey its distinctive floral-bitter aroma. Used as a finishing drizzle here."},{"term":"Planteray dark rum","pronunciation":"PLAN-teh-ray","note":"Formerly Plantation Rum — rebranded to Planteray in 2024. French-Caribbean aged rum from Maison Ferrand, known for its dual aging: first in the tropics, then finished in cognac barrels in France. Rich, smooth, and molasses-forward."},{"term":"Mascarpone","pronunciation":"mas-car-POH-neh","note":"Italian triple-cream fresh cheese from Lombardy — thick, rich, mildly sweet. The base of the tiramisu cream layer."}],"allergens":["dairy","gluten","egg"],"pron":"tee-rah-mee-SOO","story":"A San Fermo riff on the Italian classic. Instead of espresso, the ladyfingers are soaked in Earl Grey from Miro Tea — Ballard's 200-variety loose leaf tea shop — which swaps coffee's bitterness for bergamot's floral citrus. Planteray dark rum in the mascarpone cream; bergamot agrumato oil at the finish.","can_modify":[],"allergen_note":null,"raw":null},{"id":"dish:pavlova","name":"Pavlova","section":"Dessert","desc":"marigold meringue, vanilla pastry cream, gently cooked Washington peaches","price":"13","ingredients":[{"note":"a meringue-based dessert with a crisp crust and soft center, named for ballerina Anna Pavlova.","pronunciation":"pav-LOH-vah","term":"Pavlova"},{"note":"whipped egg whites and sugar, baked low and slow until crisp.","term":"Meringue","pronunciation":"meh-RANG"},{"term":"Marigold sugar","pronunciation":null,"note":"marigold petals from Rain Dog Farm in Redmond, dried and ground with sugar to flavor the meringue."},{"note":"Pastry cream is a cooked custard thickened with cornstarch, giving it a stable, spoonable texture that holds its shape on the plate. Ours is made from whole milk, cream, egg yolk, sugar, vanilla bean, and cornstarch. The vanilla bean is visible as small specks throughout. It's cool and rich, and sits underneath the meringue and peaches.","pronunciation":null,"term":"Vanilla bean pastry cream"}],"allergens":["egg","dairy"],"pron":"pav-LOH-vah","story":"A crisp-outside, soft-inside meringue, named for the Russian ballerina Anna Pavlova. This one's built on marigold sugar — petals from Rain Dog Farm in Redmond, dried and ground with sugar — flavoring the meringue base. Topped with vanilla bean pastry cream and gently cooked local Washington peaches. Floral, bright, and delicate.","can_modify":["dairy"],"allergen_note":"No flour in the meringue — gluten-free as built. Egg is in the meringue itself. The vanilla bean pastry cream carries the dairy and can ride on the side for a dairy-lighter plate.","raw":false},{"id":"dish:seasonal-gelato","name":"Seasonal Gelato","section":"Dessert","desc":"ask the kitchen for tonight's flavor","price":"8","ingredients":null,"allergens":["dairy"],"pron":null,"story":null,"can_modify":[],"allergen_note":"Flavors rotate nightly — confirm eggs or nuts with the kitchen before promising anything.","raw":null}],"service":{"intro":"San Fermo runs 85 seats (57 indoor + 28 patio), 5pm–10pm, three turns a night (T1 5–7, T2 7–9, T3 9–close). The menu is small and changes daily — the chef asks that each table puts its whole order in at once.","steps":[{"n":1,"title":"Greet within 2 minutes","detail":"Welcome the table, offer water (still or sparkling), and set the menu format: a small menu that changes daily, and the chef asks for the whole order at once."},{"n":2,"title":"Set the pace","detail":"Every table, every time — don't assume they'll volunteer it. As the greeting lands, ask: \"So I can time your courses right — are we settling in for the evening, or keeping an eye on the clock tonight?\" Settling in gets a little air between courses; on the clock gets a tight fire and a word to expo. The whole order goes in at once and the kitchen stacks up fast — nobody tells you they're in a hurry until they already are."},{"n":3,"title":"Drinks","detail":"Take the cocktail and wine order. Guide by-the-glass, mention what's notable tonight, and flag anything 86'd before they decide."},{"n":4,"title":"Full food order at once","detail":"Per the chef's request, take the entire food order in one pass. Note allergies and call out raw / under-cooked items (marked * on the menu)."},{"n":5,"title":"Fire & pace","detail":"Coordinate firing and pacing with expo. Read the table — match the pace to their turn so the next seating lands clean."},{"n":6,"title":"Mark & maintain","detail":"Mark the table for each course, crumb between courses, keep water and wine topped, and reset silver as needed."},{"n":7,"title":"Check back","detail":"Quality check two bites into each course. Catch problems early, never at the end."},{"n":8,"title":"Dessert & digestivi","detail":"Offer dessert, then the after-dinner list — amaro flights, grappa, port, and the digestivi selection."},{"n":9,"title":"Present the check","detail":"If asked about the 23% service charge: it replaces tipping, 100% goes to the house, and no additional tip is expected."},{"n":10,"title":"Reset","detail":"Reset the table promptly and cleanly for the next turn."}],"standards":[{"title":"Service charge","body":"We've eliminated tipping. A 23% service fee applies to food and beverage; 100% goes to the house. Deliver this plainly and confidently if a guest asks — it's the model, not an apology."},{"title":"Wine service","body":"Present the bottle to the host, pour a taste, then pour clockwise around the table, women first where it reads naturally, host last. Whites and bubbles in the chiller; reds at cellar temp. Top thoughtfully — don't empty the bottle without offering another."},{"title":"Allergies & raw items","body":"Items marked * are served raw or under-cooked. Always surface allergies to the kitchen at order, and repeat them on the ticket. When in doubt, ask the chef — never guess."}],"seat_note":"Seat positions traced from floorplan.png (OpenTable). p = side (t/b/l/r), o = order along that side, 0 first. Seat 1 is the pivot point."},"somm":{"sets":[{"id":"faults.descriptor","concept":"somm.faults.descriptor","tier":"SECONDARY","source":"AWRI","stem":"A bottle smells of {k}. What is the fault?","why":"{v}. (AWRI)","items":[{"k":"musty, mouldy, damp cardboard","v":"Cork taint (TCA)"},{"k":"rotten egg, onion, cooked cabbage","v":"Reduction"},{"k":"bruised apple, sherry, wet wool, varnish","v":"Oxidation"},{"k":"vinegar and nail polish","v":"Volatile acidity"},{"k":"Band-Aid, medicinal, horsey, clove","v":"Brettanomyces"}]},{"id":"faults.threshold","concept":"somm.faults.threshold","tier":"SECONDARY","source":"AWRI","stem":"{k}","why":"{v}","items":[{"k":"At what level can a trained taster detect TCA?","v":"1.4 ng/L — and guests only reject it at 3.1 ng/L. You have to catch it well below what a guest would notice.","a":"1.4 ng/L","wrong":["3.1 ng/L","14 ng/L","0.4 ng/L"]},{"k":"Volatile acidity: at what level does it become detrimental?","v":"Above 0.7 g/L. The aroma threshold is far lower, 0.1–0.125 g/L.","a":"Above 0.7 g/L","wrong":["Above 0.1 g/L","Above 1.4 g/L","Above 3 g/L"]},{"k":"Which fault blows off with aggressive aeration?","v":"Reduction. TCA does not — that single test separates the two faults most often confused.","a":"Reduction","wrong":["Cork taint (TCA)","Oxidation","Brettanomyces"]}],"choices_from":["1.4 ng/L","3.1 ng/L","0.7 g/L","Above 0.7 g/L","Reduction","Cork taint (TCA)","Oxidation","Brettanomyces","0.1–0.125 g/L"]},{"id":"temp.style","concept":"somm.service.temperature","tier":"SECONDARY","source":"CMS Europe Standards of Service","stem":"What is the serving temperature for {k}?","why":"{v}. (CMS Europe)","items":[{"k":"Champagne and sparkling","v":"6–10°C"},{"k":"light whites and rosé","v":"7–10°C"},{"k":"heavy whites and light reds","v":"10–13°C"},{"k":"medium and full-bodied reds","v":"15–18°C"},{"k":"sweet whites and dry sherry","v":"6–8°C"}]},{"id":"decant.timing","concept":"somm.service.decanting","tier":"SECONDARY","source":"Jancis Robinson","stem":"When do you decant {k}?","why":"{v}.","items":[{"k":"a red Bordeaux or Rhône over 20 years old","v":"Immediately before serving — an old, fragile wine decanted early collapses"},{"k":"a Bordeaux or Rhône of 5 to 20 years","v":"One to two hours ahead"},{"k":"a young Barolo, Barbaresco or Brunello","v":"Up to four hours ahead, splash-decanted"}]},{"id":"forbes.timing","concept":"service.luxury.timing","tier":"SECONDARY","source":"Forbes Travel Guide","stem":"Forbes Travel Guide standard: {k}","why":"{v}. (Forbes Travel Guide)","items":[{"k":"how soon must a seated table be greeted?","v":"Within one minute. San Fermo's own standard is two — a known and deliberate difference","a":"Within one minute","wrong":["Within two minutes","Within five minutes","Thirty seconds"]},{"k":"how soon must a vacated table be cleared?","v":"Within five minutes","a":"Within five minutes","wrong":["Within one minute","Within two minutes","Within ten minutes"]},{"k":"how long may a caller be left on hold without offering a call-back?","v":"Thirty seconds","a":"Thirty seconds","wrong":["One minute","Two minutes","Five minutes"]},{"k":"when must a reservation be confirmed?","v":"Within two hours of the call","a":"Within two hours of the call","wrong":["Within an hour","By the end of the day","The day before"]}],"choices_from":["Within one minute","Within two minutes","Within five minutes","Thirty seconds","Within two hours of the call","Within ten minutes","Within an hour"]},{"id":"ratings.measure","concept":"service.luxury.ratings","tier":"SECONDARY","source":"Michelin, AAA, Forbes Travel Guide","stem":"{k}","why":"{v}","items":[{"k":"Do Michelin stars take service into account?","v":"No. Michelin's own words: \"We award MICHELIN Stars for the food on the plate — nothing more.\" Comfort is the fork-and-spoon symbol, not the star.","a":"No — stars are for the food alone","wrong":["Yes, service is one of the five criteria","Yes, but only at two and three stars","Only the room, not the staff"]},{"k":"Roughly how much of a Forbes score is service rather than the room?","v":"About three quarters. Forbes-attributed figures range from 67% to 75% service — the exact number is contested, the weighting is not.","a":"About three quarters","wrong":["About half","About a quarter","Almost none — it is mostly the room"]},{"k":"What three things does AAA score a restaurant on?","v":"Food, service and vibe — AAA includes décor in the rating, where Forbes separates facility out.","a":"Food, service and vibe","wrong":["Food and service only","Food, service, décor and price","Food, wine list and service"]}],"choices_from":["No — stars are for the food alone","Yes, service is one of the five criteria","About three quarters","About half","About a quarter","Food, service and vibe","Food and service only","Food, service, décor and price"]},{"id":"wa.law","concept":"service.law.wa","tier":"PRIMARY","source":"WAC 314-11-035, WA LCB","stem":"{k}","why":"{v}","items":[{"k":"A guest is apparently intoxicated. What does Washington law require?","v":"Stop serving AND stop them drinking what is already in front of them. WAC 314-11-035 is a two-part duty, and servers carry third-party civil liability here.","a":"Stop serving and stop them drinking what they already have","wrong":["Stop serving but they may finish their drink","Serve water and slow the pours","Cut them off after one more drink"]},{"k":"By when must a new hire hold a MAST permit?","v":"Within 60 days of starting.","a":"Within 60 days of starting","wrong":["Within 30 days of starting","Within 90 days of starting","Before the first shift"]},{"k":"What is the difference between a Class 12 and a Class 13 MAST permit?","v":"Class 12 is 21+ — bartending, mixing, tastings, managing. Class 13 is 18–20 — taking orders and pouring at tables.","a":"Class 12 is 21+, Class 13 is 18–20","wrong":["Class 12 is 18–20, Class 13 is 21+","Class 12 is for managers only","Class 13 lets you bartend, Class 12 does not"]}],"choices_from":["Stop serving and stop them drinking what they already have","Stop serving but they may finish their drink","Within 60 days of starting","Within 30 days of starting","Before the first shift","Class 12 is 21+, Class 13 is 18–20","Class 12 is 18–20, Class 13 is 21+"]},{"id":"bottle.mechanics","concept":"somm.service.bottle","tier":"SECONDARY","source":"CMS Americas 2025, CMS Europe 24.1","stem":"{k}","why":"{v}","items":[{"k":"Where do you cut the foil?","v":"Below the second (lower) lip — so wine cannot run back under the foil and contaminate later pours.","a":"Below the second lip","wrong":["Above the lip","At the neck","Halfway down the foil"]},{"k":"How large is the taste pour?","v":"1 to 1.5 oz, 30–45 mL. (CMS Americas 2025.)","a":"1 to 1.5 oz","wrong":["Half an ounce","3 to 4 oz","Half a glass"]},{"k":"What is the taste pour actually for?","v":"Checking for a fault, usually cork taint. It is not a preference check.","a":"Checking for a fault","wrong":["Checking the guest likes it","Checking the temperature","Checking the vintage matches"]},{"k":"A guest's wine arrives under screwcap. Do you present the closure?","v":"No. The cork is presented; a screwcap is not.","a":"No — the cork is presented, a screwcap is not","wrong":["Yes, always present the closure","Only if the guest asks","Yes, on bottles over $100"]},{"k":"How full do you pour a glass of still wine?","v":"One third to one half, depending on the stemware. Sparkling goes to three quarters, maximum two pours per glass.","a":"One third to one half","wrong":["Three quarters","To the widest part of the bowl","A quarter"]}],"choices_from":["Below the second lip","Above the lip","At the neck","1 to 1.5 oz","3 to 4 oz","Half a glass","Checking for a fault","Checking the guest likes it","Checking the temperature","No — the cork is presented, a screwcap is not","Yes, always present the closure","One third to one half","Three quarters","To the widest part of the bowl"]},{"id":"decant.why","concept":"somm.service.decanting","tier":"SECONDARY","source":"CMS Americas 2025","stem":"{k}","why":"{v}","items":[{"k":"Name the three reasons CMS gives for decanting.","v":"Aeration, sediment removal, and raising the temperature by 1–3 degrees. The third is rarely taught and is the one that fixes a red that came up too cold.","a":"Aeration, sediment, and raising temperature","wrong":["Aeration and sediment only","Aeration, sediment, and chilling","Aeration, sediment, and showmanship"]},{"k":"The bottle has been standing upright. Do you use a cradle?","v":"No — keep it upright. A cradle is for a bottle that has been lying horizontal, so the sediment can resettle.","a":"No — keep it upright","wrong":["Yes, always cradle","Yes, if it is over ten years old","Only for Barolo"]},{"k":"When double decanting, what do you rinse the original bottle with?","v":"Similar wine, never water.","a":"Similar wine, never water","wrong":["Water","Nothing — return it as is","Soda water"]}],"choices_from":["Aeration, sediment, and raising temperature","Aeration and sediment only","Aeration, sediment, and chilling","No — keep it upright","Yes, always cradle","Similar wine, never water","Water","Nothing — return it as is"]},{"id":"floor.sales","concept":"somm.floor.sales","tier":"SECONDARY","source":"SevenFifty Daily, Decanter","stem":"{k}","why":"{v}","items":[{"k":"A guest won't name a budget. What does the trade's most-cited technique do?","v":"Hover a finger over different price points on the list and read the body language — no number is ever said aloud. That is Linda Milagros Violago, who runs the wine program at Canlis. Note there is a real second school that says just ask; both are defensible.","a":"Point at price points on the list and read the reaction","wrong":["Ask them directly what they want to spend","Recommend the middle of the list","Start at the top and come down"]},{"k":"What is bracketing?","v":"Offering three — high, middle, and one genuinely affordable — and letting the guest choose. Operators report guests trade up under a spread like that, though that is an assertion rather than a measured result.","a":"Offering three options — high, middle and affordable","wrong":["Offering only the two most expensive","Offering two — one high, one low","Asking the guest to name three they like"]},{"k":"Does classical pairing theory override what a guest asks for?","v":"No. CMS Americas is explicit that the primary goal of any pairing is the guest's preference — red with shellfish, if that is what they want.","a":"No — the guest's preference wins","wrong":["Yes — the classical pairing is correct","Yes, unless the guest insists","Only if the sommelier agrees"]}],"choices_from":["Point at price points on the list and read the reaction","Ask them directly what they want to spend","Recommend the middle of the list","Offering three options — high, middle and affordable","Offering only the two most expensive","No — the guest's preference wins","Yes — the classical pairing is correct"]}],"economics":[{"concept":"wine.econ.pour","tier":"SECONDARY","source":"US Foods","q":"How many 5oz pours come out of a 750ml bottle?","a":"5","choices":["4","5","6","7"],"why":"750ml is 25.36oz — five 5oz pours, or four at 6oz. That one-pour difference is roughly a 20% swing in revenue per bottle, which makes pour size the largest single lever in a by-the-glass programme. San Fermo currently pours four."},{"concept":"wine.econ.pour","tier":"HOUSE","source":"San Fermo wine-inventory.json","q":"How many glasses does San Fermo currently pour from a bottle?","a":"4","choices":["3","4","5","6"],"why":"Four — a 6.35oz pour. Going to five would drop every glass cost percentage by about a fifth."}]}};
